( '6.1', 'CASE 6.1.039', 'Physical Form and Asset Classification', 'Consider a hospital trust that owns its diagnostic equipment while settling invoices from medical suppliers on standard credit terms. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Although an import licence cannot be touched, it appears among non-current assets rather than current assets because of its long-term value.', 'The same server rack must always be classified identically on every balance sheet regardless of how it is held.', 'Classifying a server rack as a non-current asset depends mainly on its purchase price rather than on management''s intended use.', 'A server rack purchased to be resold still counts among non-current assets as long as it remains unsold for several months.', 'A server rack used daily in a business''s own operations should be recorded as inventory because it depreciates like stock.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Long-term value places an intangible import licence among non-current rather than current assets.', 'FALSE — Classification of the server rack depends on whether it is used or held for resale, so identical items can differ across balance sheets.', 'FALSE — Intended use, not purchase price, drives non-current classification of the server rack.', 'FALSE — Resale intent keeps the server rack in inventory as a current asset regardless of how long it remains unsold.', 'FALSE — Continued operational use makes the server rack a non-current asset; inventory is reserved for goods held for resale.'], '5/5', 39, 'full' ),
( '6.1', 'CASE 6.1.040', 'Operating Cycle and Current Assets', 'Analyze how a tractor used on a farm is classified compared with one held by an equipment dealer. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Once a dealer sells a server rack from its showroom, the buyer must continue to record it as inventory.', 'A server rack converted from resale stock into equipment used in operations keeps its original inventory classification permanently.', 'An import licence is recorded as an intangible non-current asset alongside tangible assets such as buildings and machinery.', 'A patent is excluded from non-current assets because it has no physical substance.', 'A patent is classified as a current asset because it is intangible and therefore easily converted into cash within a year.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — A buyer intending to use the server rack in operations records it as a non-current asset, not inventory, after purchase.', 'FALSE — A change in intended use reclassifies the server rack from inventory to a non-current asset.', 'TRUE — Intangible and tangible non-current assets, including an import licence, sit together in the same balance sheet section.', 'FALSE — Physical substance is not required for non-current classification; a patent qualifies through its long-term value.', 'FALSE — A patent is a non-current intangible asset because it provides benefit over several years, not because it converts to cash quickly.'], '2/5', 40, 'full' ),
( '6.1', 'CASE 6.1.041', 'Benefit Period and Non-Current Assets', 'Review how a server rack used to run internal systems is classified as a tangible non-current asset. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because a patent cannot be touched, it is recorded as an expense rather than as an asset on the balance sheet.', 'Non-current assets can never include a patent because that section is reserved strictly for tangible items like machinery.', 'A patent is treated as inventory because it is intended for use rather than for physical display.', 'A patent loses its non-current classification as soon as the business begins actively using it.', 'The absence of physical form does not prevent an import licence from being classified as a non-current asset.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — A patent with expected long-term benefit is recorded as an intangible non-current asset, not expensed immediately.', 'FALSE — The non-current assets section includes intangible items such as a patent alongside tangible assets.', 'FALSE — Long-term exclusive use, not physical display, places a patent among non-current intangible assets, not inventory.', 'FALSE — Active use does not remove a patent''s non-current classification; it confirms the long-term benefit that justifies it.', 'TRUE — Physical form is not a requirement for non-current classification of an import licence.'], '3/5', 41, 'full' ),
