Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The share price rose from €20 to €21, an increase of more than 20%.', 'With 687,000 shares outstanding at a closing price of €21, market capitalisation exceeds €11 million.', 'The last closing price of €21 is more than 50% above the first closing price of €20.', 'With operating result of €274 thousand and 687,000 shares outstanding, earnings per share exceeds €0.35.', 'The number of shares outstanding is 687,000, which is positive.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['FALSE — Price change is about 5.0%.', 'TRUE — Market capitalisation is about €14.4 million.', 'FALSE — The price rise compared with the 50% threshold.', 'TRUE — Earnings per share is about €0.40.', 'TRUE — Shares outstanding equal 687,000.'], '5/5', 125, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.001', 'Balance Sheet as a Point-in-Time Snapshot', 'Review the balance sheet as a snapshot of a business''s assets, equity and liabilities on one specific date. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A financial statement typically brings together a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss, and a cash flow statement to describe a business''s position, performance and cash movements.', 'The balance sheet is drawn up at a single point in time, whereas the statement of profit and loss and the cash flow statement each cover a period.', 'Revenue earned and costs incurred over an accounting period are reported in the statement of profit and loss rather than in the balance sheet.', 'Depreciation reflects the gradual wearing out of a fixed asset as it is used to generate revenue over its useful life.', 'A fixed asset that is never depreciated would remain on the accounts at its original cost even after years of use, overstating its true worth.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — These three statements together describe financial position, trading performance and cash movements more fully than any one alone.', 'TRUE — Only the balance sheet is a snapshot on one date; the other two statements summarise activity across an accounting period.', 'TRUE — The statement of profit and loss, not the balance sheet, records revenue and costs accumulated over the period.', 'TRUE — Depreciation allocates the loss of value that a fixed asset experiences through use over its useful life.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset stays at original cost in the accounts despite having lost value through use, which overstates it.'], '4/5', 1, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.002', 'Profit and Loss Over an Accounting Period', 'Analyze why the balance sheet does not report the value of sales made during the year. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Depreciation is charged as an expense in the statement of profit and loss without itself triggering a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'A financial statement is limited to a balance sheet alone and never includes a statement of profit and loss or a cash flow statement.', 'Under the straight-line method, an equal amount of depreciation is charged in each year of an asset''s useful life, so its book value falls steadily.', 'The balance sheet, like the statement of profit and loss and the cash flow statement, is drawn up to cover an entire accounting period rather than a single point in time.', 'Revenue earned and costs incurred over an accounting period are reported in the balance sheet rather than in the statement of profit and loss.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the related cash was paid when the asset was originally bought, not when the charge is recorded.', 'FALSE — A financial statement normally includes a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss and a cash flow statement together.', 'TRUE — Straight-line depreciation charges the same amount every year of useful life, producing a steady annual fall in book value.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet is drawn up at a single point in time; it is the other two statements that cover a period.', 'FALSE — Revenue and costs for the period are reported in the statement of profit and loss, not the balance sheet.'], '5/5', 2, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.003', 'Components of a Financial Statement', 'Consider a construction firm that finances major machinery purchases with a mix of retained profits and long-term bank borrowing. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Depreciation has nothing to do with the wearing out of a fixed asset and is simply an arbitrary bookkeeping entry with no economic meaning.', 'A fixed asset that is never depreciated will automatically show a reduced value in the accounts that reflects its true worth after years of use.', 'Land is generally treated differently from buildings, machinery and vehicles because it does not wear out through use and is normally not depreciated.', 'A profit earned during the year increases the equity reported on the balance sheet, usually by adding to retained earnings.', 'A loss incurred during the year reduces the equity reported on the balance sheet.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, true, true], ARRAY['FALSE — Depreciation specifically reflects the loss of value a fixed asset experiences through use over its useful life.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset remains at its original cost in the accounts, overstating rather than reflecting its true worth.', 'TRUE — Because land does not wear out through use in the way other fixed assets do, it is typically excluded from depreciation.', 'TRUE — Profit for the year raises equity, typically because it is added to retained earnings.', 'TRUE — A loss for the year lowers retained earnings and therefore reduces total equity.'], '2/5', 3, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.004', 'Cash Flow From Operating Activities', 'Analyze why the statement of profit and loss covers a period rather than a single point in time. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Profit for the year and the change in cash and cash equivalents for the year are two different measures that will not usually be equal.', 'Depreciation is a cash expense in the statement of profit and loss that triggers an actual cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'Cash flow from operating activities reflects cash movements arising from the core trading activities of a business during the period.', 'Cash flow from investing activities reflects cash movements arising from buying or selling long-term assets during the period.', 'Under the straight-line method, the amount of depreciation charged changes unpredictably from year to year rather than remaining equal.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Profit and the net change in cash are distinct measures and typically differ from one another.', 'FALSE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; it does not itself trigger a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'TRUE — Operating cash flow captures cash generated or used by a business''s day-to-day trading.', 'TRUE — Investing cash flow captures cash spent on, or received from, long-term assets such as equipment.', 'FALSE — Straight-line depreciation charges an equal amount every year of useful life rather than a fluctuating amount.'], '2/5', 4, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.005', 'Cash Flow From Investing Activities', 'Review how a financial statement typically combines a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss and a cash flow statement. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Cash flow from financing activities reflects cash movements arising from borrowing, repaying loans, or transactions with owners during the period.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities in a given year often signals that a business has been purchasing long-term assets rather than facing financial difficulty.', 'Land is treated in exactly the same way as buildings, machinery and vehicles and is depreciated over an estimated useful life.', 'The net change in cash and cash equivalents for a period is calculated by adding together cash flows from operating, investing and financing activities.', 'The balance sheet of a bakery shows its assets, equity and liabilities at a single point in time and does not report the value of sales made during the year.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Financing cash flow captures how a business raises and repays funding, including loans and owner transactions.', 'TRUE — Investing outflows commonly reflect spending on long-term assets rather than an indication of financial trouble.', 'FALSE — Land is generally not depreciated because it does not wear out through use in the way buildings and machinery do.', 'TRUE — Summing the operating, investing and financing cash flows gives the net change in cash for the period.', 'TRUE — A balance sheet is a snapshot on one date; sales for the year are reported in the statement of profit and loss, not the balance sheet.'], '2/5', 5, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.006', 'Cash Flow From Financing Activities', 'Analyze the purpose of the cash flow statement alongside the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because a bakery draws up its balance sheet at one specific date each year, that document alone cannot reveal how much revenue the business generated over the preceding accounting period.', 'A profit earned during the year reduces the equity reported on the balance sheet.', 'The balance sheet of a courier firm shows its assets, equity and liabilities at a single point in time and does not report the value of sales made during the year.', 'The statement of profit and loss prepared by a bakery reports the revenues, costs and resulting profit or loss earned over the accounting period, rather than at a single moment.', 'Over each financial year, a bakery records its revenues and costs in the statement of profit and loss in order to arrive at the profit or loss for that period.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Revenue earned over a period is reported in the statement of profit and loss; the balance sheet only reflects the position on its stated date.', 'FALSE — Profit for the year increases equity, typically through retained earnings, rather than reducing it.', 'TRUE — A balance sheet is a snapshot on one date; sales for the year are reported in the statement of profit and loss, not the balance sheet.', 'TRUE — Unlike the balance sheet, the statement of profit and loss covers a whole trading period rather than one specific date.', 'TRUE — The statement of profit and loss summarises revenues and costs across the accounting period to calculate the resulting profit or loss.'], '5/5', 6, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.007', 'Negative Investing Cash Flow Explained', 'Consider a furniture maker upgrading its woodworking machinery and preparing its year-end accounts. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The statement of profit and loss prepared by a courier firm reports the revenues, costs and resulting profit or loss earned over the accounting period, rather than at a single moment.', 'Over each financial year, a courier firm records its revenues and costs in the statement of profit and loss in order to arrive at the profit or loss for that period.', 'When a bakery collects payment from a customer who owed money on a trade receivable, the resulting cash inflow is classified within cash flow from operating activities.', 'If a bakery repays part of a long-term bank loan during the year, that cash outflow is classified within cash flow from financing activities rather than operating activities.', 'A cash outflow that a bakery incurs when purchasing new commercial ovens is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Unlike the balance sheet, the statement of profit and loss covers a whole trading period rather than one specific date.', 'TRUE — The statement of profit and loss summarises revenues and costs across the accounting period to calculate the resulting profit or loss.', 'TRUE — Collecting a trade receivable relates to core trading activity, so the cash inflow belongs in the operating section of the cash flow statement.', 'TRUE — Loan repayments relate to how the business is financed, so they appear in the financing section rather than the operating section.', 'TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as commercial ovens is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.'], '3/5', 7, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.008', 'Collecting a Trade Receivable', 'Analyze why failing to record depreciation would overstate the value of a fixed asset in the accounts. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A loss incurred during the year increases the equity reported on the balance sheet.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a bakery in a year when it purchased new commercial ovens often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'Profit for the year and the change in cash and cash equivalents for the year are always identical figures.', 'Cash flow from operating activities reflects cash movements arising from borrowing and repaying loans during the period.', 'Cash flow from investing activities reflects cash movements arising from the core trading activities of a business during the period.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — A loss for the year reduces equity by lowering retained earnings; it does not increase equity.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like commercial ovens, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'FALSE — Profit and the net change in cash are different measures and will not usually be identical.', 'FALSE — Borrowing and repaying loans are financing activities; operating cash flow reflects core trading instead.', 'FALSE — Core trading activities are reflected in operating cash flow; investing cash flow relates to long-term assets.'], '2/5', 8, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.009', 'Repaying a Long-Term Loan', 'Review why depreciation is described as an expense that does not by itself cause a cash payment. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A cash outflow that a courier firm incurs when purchasing new delivery van is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a courier firm in a year when it purchased new delivery van often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'Cash flow from financing activities reflects cash movements arising from buying or selling long-term assets during the period.', 'A cash outflow that a hotel chain incurs when purchasing new kitchen equipment is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities in a given year is always a definite sign that a business is facing financial difficulty.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as delivery van is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like delivery van, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'FALSE — Buying or selling long-term assets is reflected in investing cash flow, not financing cash flow.', 'TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as kitchen equipment is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.', 'FALSE — Investing outflows often simply reflect spending on long-term assets rather than financial distress.'], '2/5', 9, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.010', 'Net Change in Cash and Cash Equivalents', 'Analyze how the straight-line method spreads the depreciable cost of an asset evenly over its useful life. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A negative cash flow from investing activities at a hotel chain in a year when it purchased new kitchen equipment often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'A cash outflow that a software developer incurs when purchasing new office computer equipment is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.', 'A cash outflow that a furniture maker incurs when purchasing new woodworking machinery is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.', 'The net change in cash and cash equivalents for a period has no relationship to cash flows from operating, investing and financing activities.', 'A cash outflow that a electronics retailer incurs when purchasing new point-of-sale tills is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like kitchen equipment, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as office computer equipment is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.', 'TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as woodworking machinery is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.', 'FALSE — The net change in cash is exactly the sum of the operating, investing and financing cash flows for the period.', 'TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as point-of-sale tills is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.'], '4/5', 10, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.011', 'Depreciation and Asset Wear', 'Consider an arable farm that owns land alongside a tractor and outbuildings, and is reviewing how each asset is treated in the accounts. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A negative cash flow from investing activities at a electronics retailer in a year when it purchased new point-of-sale tills often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'The balance sheet of a bakery reports the total value of sales made throughout the year, in addition to its assets, equity and liabilities.', 'As a bakery uses its commercial ovens over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'Because a bakery draws up its balance sheet at one specific date, that same document also summarises the revenue earned across the whole financial year.', 'If a bakery failed to record depreciation on its commercial ovens, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like point-of-sale tills, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet does not report sales or turnover for the year; that information is presented in the statement of profit and loss.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like commercial ovens lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet only reflects the business''s position on its date; revenue earned across the year appears in the statement of profit and loss instead.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as commercial ovens would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.'], '5/5', 11, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.012', 'Depreciation as a Non-Cash Expense', 'Analyze why land is generally treated differently from buildings, machinery and vehicles when it comes to depreciation. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Depreciation charged on a bakery''s commercial ovens is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'Under the straight-line method, a bakery spreads the depreciable cost of its commercial ovens evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.', 'As a courier firm uses its delivery van over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'If a courier firm failed to record depreciation on its delivery van, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.', 'If a hotel chain failed to record depreciation on its kitchen equipment, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the commercial ovens was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on commercial ovens in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like delivery van lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as delivery van would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as kitchen equipment would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.'], '5/5', 12, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.013', 'Straight-Line Depreciation Method', 'Review how a profit earned during the year affects the equity reported on the balance sheet. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Depreciation charged on a hotel chain''s kitchen equipment is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'The balance sheet of a courier firm reports the total value of sales made throughout the year, in addition to its assets, equity and liabilities.', 'Under the straight-line method, a hotel chain spreads the depreciable cost of its kitchen equipment evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.', 'As a software developer uses its office computer equipment over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'Because a courier firm draws up its balance sheet at one specific date, that same document also summarises the revenue earned across the whole financial year.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the kitchen equipment was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet does not report sales or turnover for the year; that information is presented in the statement of profit and loss.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on kitchen equipment in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like office computer equipment lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet only reflects the business''s position on its date; revenue earned across the year appears in the statement of profit and loss instead.'], '5/5', 13, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.014', 'Book Value After Depreciation', 'Analyze how a loss incurred during the year affects the equity reported on the balance sheet. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a software developer failed to record depreciation on its office computer equipment, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.', 'Depreciation charged on a software developer''s office computer equipment is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'Under the straight-line method, a software developer spreads the depreciable cost of its office computer equipment evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.', 'The balance sheet of a construction firm reports the total value of sales made throughout the year, in addition to its assets, equity and liabilities.', 'Because a construction firm draws up its balance sheet at one specific date, that same document also summarises the revenue earned across the whole financial year.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as office computer equipment would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.', 'TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the office computer equipment was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on office computer equipment in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet does not report sales or turnover for the year; that information is presented in the statement of profit and loss.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet only reflects the business''s position on its date; revenue earned across the year appears in the statement of profit and loss instead.'], '2/5', 14, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.015', 'Land and Non-Depreciable Assets', 'Consider a pharmacy chain reviewing why its reported profit for the year does not match the change in its cash balance. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The balance sheet of a hotel chain reports the total value of sales made throughout the year, in addition to its assets, equity and liabilities.', 'Because a hotel chain draws up its balance sheet at one specific date, that same document also summarises the revenue earned across the whole financial year.', 'As a garage uses its diagnostic equipment over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'If a garage failed to record depreciation on its diagnostic equipment, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.', 'Depreciation charged on a garage''s diagnostic equipment is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, true, true], ARRAY['FALSE — The balance sheet does not report sales or turnover for the year; that information is presented in the statement of profit and loss.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet only reflects the business''s position on its date; revenue earned across the year appears in the statement of profit and loss instead.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like diagnostic equipment lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as diagnostic equipment would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.', 'TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the diagnostic equipment was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.'], '5/5', 15, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.016', 'Profit Raises Equity', 'Analyze how non-cash items such as depreciation cause profit and cash flow to diverge. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Under the straight-line method, a garage spreads the depreciable cost of its diagnostic equipment evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.', 'As a furniture maker uses its woodworking machinery over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'If a furniture maker failed to record depreciation on its woodworking machinery, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.', 'Depreciation charged on a furniture maker''s woodworking machinery is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'If a electronics retailer failed to record depreciation on its point-of-sale tills, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on diagnostic equipment in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like woodworking machinery lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as woodworking machinery would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.', 'TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the woodworking machinery was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as point-of-sale tills would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.'], '5/5', 16, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.017', 'Loss Reduces Equity', 'Review the three sections of a cash flow statement: operating, investing and financing activities. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Depreciation charged on a electronics retailer''s point-of-sale tills is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'The balance sheet of a software developer reports the total value of sales made throughout the year, in addition to its assets, equity and liabilities.', 'Because a software developer draws up its balance sheet at one specific date, that same document also summarises the revenue earned across the whole financial year.', 'Under the straight-line method, a electronics retailer spreads the depreciable cost of its point-of-sale tills evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.', 'The statement of profit and loss prepared by a bakery is drawn up at a single point in time and does not cover an accounting period.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the point-of-sale tills was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet does not report sales or turnover for the year; that information is presented in the statement of profit and loss.', 'FALSE — The balance sheet only reflects the business''s position on its date; revenue earned across the year appears in the statement of profit and loss instead.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on point-of-sale tills in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.', 'FALSE — It is the balance sheet that is drawn up at a single point in time; the statement of profit and loss covers a period.'], '5/5', 17, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.018', 'Profit Versus Cash Flow', 'Analyze which section of the cash flow statement reflects cash movements from core trading activities. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Land owned by a bakery is generally not depreciated, because unlike buildings, machinery or vehicles, it does not wear out through use over time.', 'A bakery''s statement of profit and loss records its assets, equity and liabilities rather than its revenues and costs for the period.', 'When a bakery earns a profit for the year, that profit increases the equity shown on its balance sheet, typically through an increase in retained earnings.', 'When a bakery makes a loss for the year, that loss reduces the equity shown on its balance sheet.', 'A bakery can report a profit for the year in its statement of profit and loss while still experiencing a fall in its cash and cash equivalents, because profit and cash flow are not the same thing.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Land normally has an unlimited useful life and is not used up in the way that buildings or machinery are, so it is typically excluded from depreciation.', 'FALSE — Assets, equity and liabilities are reported on the balance sheet; the statement of profit and loss records revenues, costs and the resulting profit or loss.', 'TRUE — Profit for the year adds to the owners'' claim on the business, raising equity, usually via retained earnings.', 'TRUE — A loss for the year reduces retained earnings and therefore lowers total equity on the balance sheet.', 'TRUE — Profit and cash movements can diverge; a business can be profitable on paper while its cash balance falls for other reasons during the year.'], '4/5', 18, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.019', 'Turnover and the Balance Sheet', 'Consider a publishing house that repaid part of a long-term loan during the year and is classifying the cash outflow correctly. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The statement of profit and loss prepared by a courier firm is drawn up at a single point in time and does not cover an accounting period.', 'Because non-cash items such as depreciation are deducted in arriving at profit, the profit reported by a bakery for the year will not usually equal the net change in its cash and cash equivalents.', 'A courier firm''s statement of profit and loss records its assets, equity and liabilities rather than its revenues and costs for the period.', 'When a bakery collects payment from a customer who owed money on a trade receivable, the resulting cash inflow is classified within cash flow from financing activities.', 'If a bakery repays part of a long-term bank loan during the year, that cash outflow is classified within cash flow from operating activities rather than financing activities.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — It is the balance sheet that is drawn up at a single point in time; the statement of profit and loss covers a period.', 'TRUE — Depreciation and other non-cash adjustments mean reported profit and the net change in cash rarely match exactly.', 'FALSE — Assets, equity and liabilities are reported on the balance sheet; the statement of profit and loss records revenues, costs and the resulting profit or loss.', 'FALSE — Collecting a trade receivable relates to core trading activity and belongs in cash flow from operating activities, not financing activities.', 'FALSE — Loan repayments relate to financing the business and belong in cash flow from financing activities, not operating activities.'], '4/5', 19, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.020', 'Revenue Recognition Over Time', 'Analyze which section of the cash flow statement reflects cash movements from borrowing or repaying loans. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['When a courier firm collects payment from a customer who owed money on a trade receivable, the resulting cash inflow is classified within cash flow from financing activities.', 'If a courier firm repays part of a long-term bank loan during the year, that cash outflow is classified within cash flow from operating activities rather than financing activities.', 'A courier firm can report a profit for the year in its statement of profit and loss while still experiencing a fall in its cash and cash equivalents, because profit and cash flow are not the same thing.', 'Because non-cash items such as depreciation are deducted in arriving at profit, the profit reported by a courier firm for the year will not usually equal the net change in its cash and cash equivalents.', 'A cash outflow that a bakery incurs when purchasing new commercial ovens is normally classified within cash flow from financing activities.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Collecting a trade receivable relates to core trading activity and belongs in cash flow from operating activities, not financing activities.', 'FALSE — Loan repayments relate to financing the business and belong in cash flow from financing activities, not operating activities.', 'TRUE — Profit and cash movements can diverge; a business can be profitable on paper while its cash balance falls for other reasons during the year.', 'TRUE — Depreciation and other non-cash adjustments mean reported profit and the net change in cash rarely match exactly.', 'FALSE — Purchasing long-term assets such as commercial ovens is an investing decision, so the cash outflow belongs in the investing section, not the financing section.'], '5/5', 20, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.021', 'Bakery Balance Sheet Snapshot', 'Review why a negative cash flow from investing activities does not necessarily indicate financial distress. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A negative cash flow from investing activities at a bakery always indicates that the business is in financial difficulty, regardless of what caused it.', 'A cash outflow that a courier firm incurs when purchasing new delivery van is normally classified within cash flow from financing activities.', 'A construction firm can report a profit for the year in its statement of profit and loss while still experiencing a fall in its cash and cash equivalents, because profit and cash flow are not the same thing.', 'A cash outflow that a hotel chain incurs when purchasing new kitchen equipment is normally classified within cash flow from financing activities.', 'A cash outflow that a furniture maker incurs when purchasing new woodworking machinery is normally classified within cash flow from financing activities.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Negative investing cash flow often simply reflects spending on new assets such as commercial ovens, not financial distress.', 'FALSE — Purchasing long-term assets such as delivery van is an investing decision, so the cash outflow belongs in the investing section, not the financing section.', 'TRUE — Profit and cash movements can diverge; a business can be profitable on paper while its cash balance falls for other reasons during the year.', 'FALSE — Purchasing long-term assets such as kitchen equipment is an investing decision, so the cash outflow belongs in the investing section, not the financing section.', 'FALSE — Purchasing long-term assets such as woodworking machinery is an investing decision, so the cash outflow belongs in the investing section, not the financing section.'], '5/5', 21, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.022', 'Courier Firm Cash Flow Classification', 'Analyze how collecting a trade receivable is classified within the cash flow statement. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because non-cash items such as depreciation are deducted in arriving at profit, the profit reported by a construction firm for the year will not usually equal the net change in its cash and cash equivalents.', 'A hotel chain can report a profit for the year in its statement of profit and loss while still experiencing a fall in its cash and cash equivalents, because profit and cash flow are not the same thing.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a furniture maker always indicates that the business is in financial difficulty, regardless of what caused it.', 'A complete financial statement for a bakery typically comprises a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss, and a cash flow statement.', 'A cash outflow that a brewery incurs when purchasing new brewing tanks is normally classified within cash flow from financing activities.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Depreciation and other non-cash adjustments mean reported profit and the net change in cash rarely match exactly.', 'TRUE — Profit and cash movements can diverge; a business can be profitable on paper while its cash balance falls for other reasons during the year.', 'FALSE — Negative investing cash flow often simply reflects spending on new assets such as woodworking machinery, not financial distress.', 'TRUE — These three statements together give a fuller picture of financial position, performance and cash movements than any one of them alone.', 'FALSE — Purchasing long-term assets such as brewing tanks is an investing decision, so the cash outflow belongs in the investing section, not the financing section.'], '5/5', 22, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.023', 'Construction Firm Machinery Investment', 'Consider a construction firm that finances major machinery purchases with a mix of retained profits and long-term bank borrowing. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A negative cash flow from investing activities at a brewery always indicates that the business is in financial difficulty, regardless of what caused it.', 'A cash outflow that a textile mill incurs when purchasing new spinning machinery is normally classified within cash flow from financing activities.', 'Alongside its balance sheet and statement of profit and loss, a bakery also prepares a cash flow statement to show how cash moved during the year.', 'A cash outflow that a electronics retailer incurs when purchasing new point-of-sale tills is normally classified within cash flow from financing activities.', 'As a bakery uses its commercial ovens over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Negative investing cash flow often simply reflects spending on new assets such as brewing tanks, not financial distress.', 'FALSE — Purchasing long-term assets such as spinning machinery is an investing decision, so the cash outflow belongs in the investing section, not the financing section.', 'TRUE — The cash flow statement complements the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss by tracking cash inflows and outflows over the period.', 'FALSE — Purchasing long-term assets such as point-of-sale tills is an investing decision, so the cash outflow belongs in the investing section, not the financing section.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as commercial ovens do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.'], '5/5', 23, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.024', 'Hotel Chain Kitchen Equipment Depreciation', 'Analyze how the net change in cash and cash equivalents is calculated from the three cash flow sections. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a bakery failed to record depreciation on its commercial ovens, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'Depreciation charged on a bakery''s commercial ovens is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.', 'Under the straight-line method, a bakery charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its commercial ovens each year rather than an equal amount.', 'As a courier firm uses its delivery van over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.', 'A complete financial statement for a courier firm typically comprises a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss, and a cash flow statement.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as commercial ovens would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the commercial ovens was purchased.', 'FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on commercial ovens every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as delivery van do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.', 'TRUE — These three statements together give a fuller picture of financial position, performance and cash movements than any one of them alone.'], '2/5', 24, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.025', 'Software Developer Profit and Cash Divergence', 'Review the distinction between a point-in-time statement and a statement covering a period. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a courier firm failed to record depreciation on its delivery van, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'Depreciation charged on a courier firm''s delivery van is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.', 'Alongside its balance sheet and statement of profit and loss, a courier firm also prepares a cash flow statement to show how cash moved during the year.', 'A complete financial statement for a construction firm typically comprises a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss, and a cash flow statement.', 'Under the straight-line method, a courier firm charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its delivery van each year rather than an equal amount.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as delivery van would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the delivery van was purchased.', 'TRUE — The cash flow statement complements the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss by tracking cash inflows and outflows over the period.', 'TRUE — These three statements together give a fuller picture of financial position, performance and cash movements than any one of them alone.', 'FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on delivery van every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.'], '5/5', 25, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.026', 'Garage Diagnostic Equipment Purchase', 'Analyze why turnover for the year appears in the statement of profit and loss rather than the balance sheet. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Alongside its balance sheet and statement of profit and loss, a construction firm also prepares a cash flow statement to show how cash moved during the year.', 'As a construction firm uses its heavy construction machinery over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.', 'A complete financial statement for a hotel chain typically comprises a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss, and a cash flow statement.', 'If a construction firm failed to record depreciation on its heavy construction machinery, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'Depreciation charged on a construction firm''s heavy construction machinery is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — The cash flow statement complements the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss by tracking cash inflows and outflows over the period.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as heavy construction machinery do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.', 'TRUE — These three statements together give a fuller picture of financial position, performance and cash movements than any one of them alone.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as heavy construction machinery would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the heavy construction machinery was purchased.'], '2/5', 26, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.027', 'Furniture Maker Depreciation Policy', 'Consider a furniture maker upgrading its woodworking machinery and preparing its year-end accounts. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Alongside its balance sheet and statement of profit and loss, a hotel chain also prepares a cash flow statement to show how cash moved during the year.', 'A complete financial statement for a software developer typically comprises a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss, and a cash flow statement.', 'The net change in cash and cash equivalents for a bakery over the year equals the sum of its cash flows from operating, investing and financing activities.', 'Because a courier firm draws up its balance sheet at one specific date each year, that document alone cannot reveal how much revenue the business generated over the preceding accounting period.', 'The balance sheet of a construction firm shows its assets, equity and liabilities at a single point in time and does not report the value of sales made during the year.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — The cash flow statement complements the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss by tracking cash inflows and outflows over the period.', 'TRUE — These three statements together give a fuller picture of financial position, performance and cash movements than any one of them alone.', 'TRUE — Adding the operating, investing and financing cash flows together gives the net change in cash and cash equivalents for the period.', 'TRUE — Revenue earned over a period is reported in the statement of profit and loss; the balance sheet only reflects the position on its stated date.', 'TRUE — A balance sheet is a snapshot on one date; sales for the year are reported in the statement of profit and loss, not the balance sheet.'], '3/5', 27, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.028', 'Brewery Investing Cash Flow', 'Analyze the difference between a cash expense and a non-cash expense such as depreciation. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because a construction firm draws up its balance sheet at one specific date each year, that document alone cannot reveal how much revenue the business generated over the preceding accounting period.', 'The balance sheet of a hotel chain shows its assets, equity and liabilities at a single point in time and does not report the value of sales made during the year.', 'The statement of profit and loss prepared by a construction firm reports the revenues, costs and resulting profit or loss earned over the accounting period, rather than at a single moment.', 'Over each financial year, a construction firm records its revenues and costs in the statement of profit and loss in order to arrive at the profit or loss for that period.', 'Under the straight-line method, a construction firm charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its heavy construction machinery each year rather than an equal amount.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Revenue earned over a period is reported in the statement of profit and loss; the balance sheet only reflects the position on its stated date.', 'TRUE — A balance sheet is a snapshot on one date; sales for the year are reported in the statement of profit and loss, not the balance sheet.', 'TRUE — Unlike the balance sheet, the statement of profit and loss covers a whole trading period rather than one specific date.', 'TRUE — The statement of profit and loss summarises revenues and costs across the accounting period to calculate the resulting profit or loss.', 'FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on heavy construction machinery every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.'], '5/5', 28, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.029', 'Textile Mill Machinery Wear', 'Review how the components of a financial statement work together to describe a business''s financial position and performance. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The statement of profit and loss prepared by a hotel chain reports the revenues, costs and resulting profit or loss earned over the accounting period, rather than at a single moment.', 'Over each financial year, a hotel chain records its revenues and costs in the statement of profit and loss in order to arrive at the profit or loss for that period.', 'When a courier firm collects payment from a customer who owed money on a trade receivable, the resulting cash inflow is classified within cash flow from operating activities.', 'If a courier firm repays part of a long-term bank loan during the year, that cash outflow is classified within cash flow from financing activities rather than operating activities.', 'As a hotel chain uses its kitchen equipment over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Unlike the balance sheet, the statement of profit and loss covers a whole trading period rather than one specific date.', 'TRUE — The statement of profit and loss summarises revenues and costs across the accounting period to calculate the resulting profit or loss.', 'TRUE — Collecting a trade receivable relates to core trading activity, so the cash inflow belongs in the operating section of the cash flow statement.', 'TRUE — Loan repayments relate to how the business is financed, so they appear in the financing section rather than the operating section.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as kitchen equipment do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.'], '4/5', 29, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.030', 'Printing Company Asset Purchase', 'Analyze why the balance sheet alone cannot show how a business performed over the whole year. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['When a construction firm collects payment from a customer who owed money on a trade receivable, the resulting cash inflow is classified within cash flow from operating activities.', 'If a construction firm repays part of a long-term bank loan during the year, that cash outflow is classified within cash flow from financing activities rather than operating activities.', 'A cash outflow that a construction firm incurs when purchasing new heavy construction machinery is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a construction firm in a year when it purchased new heavy construction machinery often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'Depreciation charged on a courier firm''s delivery van is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Collecting a trade receivable relates to core trading activity, so the cash inflow belongs in the operating section of the cash flow statement.', 'TRUE — Loan repayments relate to how the business is financed, so they appear in the financing section rather than the operating section.', 'TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as heavy construction machinery is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like heavy construction machinery, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the delivery van was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.'], '5/5', 30, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.031', 'Arable Farm Land and Tractor', 'Consider an arable farm that owns land alongside a tractor and outbuildings, and is reviewing how each asset is treated in the accounts. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a hotel chain failed to record depreciation on its kitchen equipment, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'Under the straight-line method, a courier firm spreads the depreciable cost of its delivery van evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.', 'As a construction firm uses its heavy construction machinery over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'If a construction firm failed to record depreciation on its heavy construction machinery, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.', 'Depreciation charged on a hotel chain''s kitchen equipment is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as kitchen equipment would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on delivery van in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like heavy construction machinery lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as heavy construction machinery would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the kitchen equipment was purchased.'], '5/5', 31, 'full' ),
