ok value falls steadily.'], '5/5', 32, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.033', 'Overstated Assets Without Depreciation', 'Review the relationship between reported loss and the equity shown on the balance sheet. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['As a hotel chain uses its kitchen equipment over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'Under the straight-line method, a furniture maker spreads the depreciable cost of its woodworking machinery evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.', 'If a brewery failed to record depreciation on its brewing tanks, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.', 'Depreciation charged on a brewery''s brewing tanks is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'Land owned by a courier firm is generally not depreciated, because unlike buildings, machinery or vehicles, it does not wear out through use over time.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Fixed assets like kitchen equipment lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on woodworking machinery in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as brewing tanks would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.', 'TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the brewing tanks was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.', 'TRUE — Land normally has an unlimited useful life and is not used up in the way that buildings or machinery are, so it is typically excluded from depreciation.'], '3/5', 33, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.034', 'Understanding the Three Cash Flow Sections', 'Analyze why a business can be profitable yet still see its cash balance fall during the year. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Land owned by a construction firm is generally not depreciated, because unlike buildings, machinery or vehicles, it does not wear out through use over time.', 'When a courier firm earns a profit for the year, that profit increases the equity shown on its balance sheet, typically through an increase in retained earnings.', 'When a courier firm makes a loss for the year, that loss reduces the equity shown on its balance sheet.', 'Because non-cash items such as depreciation are deducted in arriving at profit, the profit reported by a hotel chain for the year will not usually equal the net change in its cash and cash equivalents.', 'A software developer can report a profit for the year in its statement of profit and loss while still experiencing a fall in its cash and cash equivalents, because profit and cash flow are not the same thing.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Land normally has an unlimited useful life and is not used up in the way that buildings or machinery are, so it is typically excluded from depreciation.', 'TRUE — Profit for the year adds to the owners'' claim on the business, raising equity, usually via retained earnings.', 'TRUE — A loss for the year reduces retained earnings and therefore lowers total equity on the balance sheet.', 'TRUE — Depreciation and other non-cash adjustments mean reported profit and the net change in cash rarely match exactly.', 'TRUE — Profit and cash movements can diverge; a business can be profitable on paper while its cash balance falls for other reasons during the year.'], '4/5', 34, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.035', 'Operating Cash Flow and Core Trading', 'Consider a pharmacy chain reviewing why its reported profit for the year does not match the change in its cash balance. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because non-cash items such as depreciation are deducted in arriving at profit, the profit reported by a software developer for the year will not usually equal the net change in its cash and cash equivalents.', 'Depreciation charged on a software developer''s office computer equipment is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.', 'A garage can report a profit for the year in its statement of profit and loss while still experiencing a fall in its cash and cash equivalents, because profit and cash flow are not the same thing.', 'Under the straight-line method, a software developer charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its office computer equipment each year rather than an equal amount.', 'As a garage uses its diagnostic equipment over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Depreciation and other non-cash adjustments mean reported profit and the net change in cash rarely match exactly.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the office computer equipment was purchased.', 'TRUE — Profit and cash movements can diverge; a business can be profitable on paper while its cash balance falls for other reasons during the year.', 'FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on office computer equipment every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as diagnostic equipment do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.'], '5/5', 35, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.036', 'Investing Cash Flow and Fixed Assets', 'Analyze why land is normally excluded from a business''s depreciation schedule. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because non-cash items such as depreciation are deducted in arriving at profit, the profit reported by a garage for the year will not usually equal the net change in its cash and cash equivalents.', 'If a garage failed to record depreciation on its diagnostic equipment, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'A furniture maker can report a profit for the year in its statement of profit and loss while still experiencing a fall in its cash and cash equivalents, because profit and cash flow are not the same thing.', 'Depreciation charged on a garage''s diagnostic equipment is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.', 'Alongside its balance sheet and statement of profit and loss, a software developer also prepares a cash flow statement to show how cash moved during the year.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Depreciation and other non-cash adjustments mean reported profit and the net change in cash rarely match exactly.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as diagnostic equipment would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'TRUE — Profit and cash movements can diverge; a business can be profitable on paper while its cash balance falls for other reasons during the year.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the diagnostic equipment was purchased.', 'TRUE — The cash flow statement complements the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss by tracking cash inflows and outflows over the period.'], '4/5', 36, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.037', 'Financing Cash Flow and Borrowing', 'Review how the useful life of a fixed asset is used to calculate straight-line depreciation. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A complete financial statement for a garage typically comprises a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss, and a cash flow statement.', 'Under the straight-line method, a garage charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its diagnostic equipment each year rather than an equal amount.', 'Alongside its balance sheet and statement of profit and loss, a garage also prepares a cash flow statement to show how cash moved during the year.', 'As a furniture maker uses its woodworking machinery over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.', 'If a furniture maker failed to record depreciation on its woodworking machinery, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — These three statements together give a fuller picture of financial position, performance and cash movements than any one of them alone.', 'FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on diagnostic equipment every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'TRUE — The cash flow statement complements the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss by tracking cash inflows and outflows over the period.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as woodworking machinery do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as woodworking machinery would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.'], '5/5', 37, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.038', 'Retained Earnings and Equity Growth', 'Analyze how accumulated depreciation reduces an asset''s book value over time. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A complete financial statement for a furniture maker typically comprises a balance sheet, a statement of profit and loss, and a cash flow statement.', 'Alongside its balance sheet and statement of profit and loss, a furniture maker also prepares a cash flow statement to show how cash moved during the year.', 'The net change in cash and cash equivalents for a courier firm over the year equals the sum of its cash flows from operating, investing and financing activities.', 'Because a hotel chain draws up its balance sheet at one specific date each year, that document alone cannot reveal how much revenue the business generated over the preceding accounting period.', 'The balance sheet of a software developer shows its assets, equity and liabilities at a single point in time and does not report the value of sales made during the year.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — These three statements together give a fuller picture of financial position, performance and cash movements than any one of them alone.', 'TRUE — The cash flow statement complements the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss by tracking cash inflows and outflows over the period.', 'TRUE — Adding the operating, investing and financing cash flows together gives the net change in cash and cash equivalents for the period.', 'TRUE — Revenue earned over a period is reported in the statement of profit and loss; the balance sheet only reflects the position on its stated date.', 'TRUE — A balance sheet is a snapshot on one date; sales for the year are reported in the statement of profit and loss, not the balance sheet.'], '5/5', 38, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.039', 'Loss and Retained Earnings Decline', 'Consider a publishing house that repaid part of a long-term loan during the year and is classifying the cash outflow correctly. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because a software developer draws up its balance sheet at one specific date each year, that document alone cannot reveal how much revenue the business generated over the preceding accounting period.', 'The balance sheet of a garage shows its assets, equity and liabilities at a single point in time and does not report the value of sales made during the year.', 'Depreciation charged on a furniture maker''s woodworking machinery is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.', 'The statement of profit and loss prepared by a software developer reports the revenues, costs and resulting profit or loss earned over the accounting period, rather than at a single moment.', 'Over each financial year, a software developer records its revenues and costs in the statement of profit and loss in order to arrive at the profit or loss for that period.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Revenue earned over a period is reported in the statement of profit and loss; the balance sheet only reflects the position on its stated date.', 'TRUE — A balance sheet is a snapshot on one date; sales for the year are reported in the statement of profit and loss, not the balance sheet.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the woodworking machinery was purchased.', 'TRUE — Unlike the balance sheet, the statement of profit and loss covers a whole trading period rather than one specific date.', 'TRUE — The statement of profit and loss summarises revenues and costs across the accounting period to calculate the resulting profit or loss.'], '3/5', 39, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.040', 'Reading a Balance Sheet Correctly', 'Analyze why financing activities in the cash flow statement often involve loans and owner transactions. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The statement of profit and loss prepared by a garage reports the revenues, costs and resulting profit or loss earned over the accounting period, rather than at a single moment.', 'Over each financial year, a garage records its revenues and costs in the statement of profit and loss in order to arrive at the profit or loss for that period.', 'When a hotel chain collects payment from a customer who owed money on a trade receivable, the resulting cash inflow is classified within cash flow from operating activities.', 'If a hotel chain repays part of a long-term bank loan during the year, that cash outflow is classified within cash flow from financing activities rather than operating activities.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a software developer in a year when it purchased new office computer equipment often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Unlike the balance sheet, the statement of profit and loss covers a whole trading period rather than one specific date.', 'TRUE — The statement of profit and loss summarises revenues and costs across the accounting period to calculate the resulting profit or loss.', 'TRUE — Collecting a trade receivable relates to core trading activity, so the cash inflow belongs in the operating section of the cash flow statement.', 'TRUE — Loan repayments relate to how the business is financed, so they appear in the financing section rather than the operating section.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like office computer equipment, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.'], '3/5', 40, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.041', 'Reading a Statement of Profit and Loss', 'Review why operating activities in the cash flow statement reflect the core trading of a business. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Under the straight-line method, a furniture maker charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its woodworking machinery each year rather than an equal amount.', 'As a brewery uses its brewing tanks over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.', 'If a brewery failed to record depreciation on its brewing tanks, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'Depreciation charged on a brewery''s brewing tanks is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.', 'A cash outflow that a garage incurs when purchasing new diagnostic equipment is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on woodworking machinery every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as brewing tanks do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as brewing tanks would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the brewing tanks was purchased.', 'TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as diagnostic equipment is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.'], '3/5', 41, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.042', 'Reading a Cash Flow Statement', 'Analyze how a business''s overall change in cash for the year links its three cash flow sections together. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Under the straight-line method, a brewery charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its brewing tanks each year rather than an equal amount.', 'As a textile mill uses its spinning machinery over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.', 'If a textile mill failed to record depreciation on its spinning machinery, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'Depreciation charged on a textile mill''s spinning machinery is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a garage in a year when it purchased new diagnostic equipment often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on brewing tanks every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as spinning machinery do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as spinning machinery would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the spinning machinery was purchased.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like diagnostic equipment, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.'], '5/5', 42, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.043', 'Fixed Assets and Useful Life', 'Consider a construction firm that finances major machinery purchases with a mix of retained profits and long-term bank borrowing. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Under the straight-line method, a textile mill charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its spinning machinery each year rather than an equal amount.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a furniture maker in a year when it purchased new woodworking machinery often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'As a printing company uses its printing press over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.', 'If a printing company failed to record depreciation on its printing press, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'Depreciation charged on a printing company''s printing press is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on spinning machinery every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like woodworking machinery, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as printing press do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as printing press would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the printing press was purchased.'], '5/5', 43, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.044', 'Accumulated Depreciation Over Time', 'Analyze why collecting money owed by a customer does not belong in the financing section of the cash flow statement. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Under the straight-line method, a printing company charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its printing press each year rather than an equal amount.', 'As a arable farm uses its tractor over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.', 'If a arable farm failed to record depreciation on its tractor, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'A cash outflow that a brewery incurs when purchasing new brewing tanks is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.', 'Depreciation charged on a arable farm''s tractor is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on printing press every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as tractor do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as tractor would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as brewing tanks is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the tractor was purchased.'], '3/5', 44, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.045', 'Depreciation Charge Consistency', 'Review why repaying a loan does not belong in the operating section of the cash flow statement. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Under the straight-line method, a arable farm charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its tractor each year rather than an equal amount.', 'As a electronics retailer uses its point-of-sale tills over time, no adjustment for wear and tear is required in the accounts because fixed assets keep their original value indefinitely.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a brewery in a year when it purchased new brewing tanks often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'If a electronics retailer failed to record depreciation on its point-of-sale tills, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be understated.', 'Depreciation charged on a electronics retailer''s point-of-sale tills is a cash expense that directly reduces the bank balance in the year it is recorded.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on tractor every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'FALSE — Fixed assets such as point-of-sale tills do wear out through use, and depreciation is charged to reflect that loss of value over time.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like brewing tanks, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'FALSE — Without depreciation, an asset such as point-of-sale tills would remain at its original cost, which overstates its value rather than understating it.', 'FALSE — Depreciation does not itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded; it simply allocates a cost that was paid when the point-of-sale tills was purchased.'], '4/5', 45, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.046', 'Financial Statement Structure Overview', 'Analyze the difference between the balance sheet''s treatment of assets and the statement of profit and loss''s treatment of costs. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A cash outflow that a textile mill incurs when purchasing new spinning machinery is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.', 'Under the straight-line method, a electronics retailer charges a different and unpredictable amount of depreciation on its point-of-sale tills each year rather than an equal amount.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a textile mill in a year when it purchased new spinning machinery often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'Land owned by a bakery is depreciated in the same way as its buildings and machinery, because all fixed assets wear out identically through use.', 'When a bakery earns a profit for the year, that profit reduces the equity shown on its balance sheet.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as spinning machinery is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.', 'FALSE — The straight-line method charges the same amount of depreciation on point-of-sale tills every year of its useful life, not a varying amount.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like spinning machinery, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'FALSE — Land is generally not depreciated because it does not wear out through use in the way buildings and machinery do.', 'FALSE — Profit for the year increases equity, typically through retained earnings, rather than reducing it.'], '5/5', 46, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.047', 'Balance Sheet Versus Income Statement', 'Consider a furniture maker upgrading its woodworking machinery and preparing its year-end accounts. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A cash outflow that a printing company incurs when purchasing new printing press is normally classified within cash flow from investing activities.', 'When a bakery makes a loss for the year, that loss increases the equity shown on its balance sheet.', 'A negative cash flow from investing activities at a printing company in a year when it purchased new printing press often simply reflects that spending rather than being a sign of financial distress.', 'As a brewery uses its brewing tanks over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'Under the straight-line method, a brewery spreads the depreciable cost of its brewing tanks evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Buying long-term assets such as printing press is an investing decision, so the related cash outflow sits in the investing section of the cash flow statement.', 'FALSE — A loss for the year reduces equity by lowering retained earnings; it does not increase equity.', 'TRUE — Negative investing cash flow frequently indicates that a business is investing in long-term assets like printing press, which is a normal and often healthy pattern.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like brewing tanks lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on brewing tanks in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.'], '4/5', 47, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.048', 'Cash Flow Statement Purpose', 'Analyze why depreciation is charged even though no cash leaves the business at the time it is recorded. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['As a textile mill uses its spinning machinery over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'If a textile mill failed to record depreciation on its spinning machinery, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.', 'When a courier firm earns a profit for the year, that profit reduces the equity shown on its balance sheet.', 'Depreciation charged on a textile mill''s spinning machinery is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'Under the straight-line method, a textile mill spreads the depreciable cost of its spinning machinery evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Fixed assets like spinning machinery lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as spinning machinery would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.', 'FALSE — Profit for the year increases equity, typically through retained earnings, rather than reducing it.', 'TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the spinning machinery was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on spinning machinery in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.'], '5/5', 48, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.049', 'Healthy Growth and Negative Investing Flow', 'Review how an asset originally recorded at cost is gradually written down through depreciation. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['When a courier firm makes a loss for the year, that loss increases the equity shown on its balance sheet.', 'When a construction firm earns a profit for the year, that profit reduces the equity shown on its balance sheet.', 'The profit reported by a bakery for the year is always identical to the net change in its cash and cash equivalents over that year.', 'As a printing company uses its printing press over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'If a printing company failed to record depreciation on its printing press, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, true, true], ARRAY['FALSE — A loss for the year reduces equity by lowering retained earnings; it does not increase equity.', 'FALSE — Profit for the year increases equity, typically through retained earnings, rather than reducing it.', 'FALSE — Profit and the net change in cash are different measures and will not usually be identical, partly because of non-cash items such as depreciation.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like printing press lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as printing press would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.'], '5/5', 49, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.050', 'Trade Receivable Cash Classification', 'Analyze why a business''s reported profit is not the same thing as cash generated during the year. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Depreciation charged on a printing company''s printing press is an expense in the statement of profit and loss that does not by itself cause a cash payment in the year it is recorded.', 'Under the straight-line method, a printing company spreads the depreciable cost of its printing press evenly over its estimated useful life, reducing the asset''s book value by the same amount each year.', 'Because depreciation is a cash expense for a bakery, its cash flow for the year will always equal its reported profit.', 'As a arable farm uses its tractor over time, the asset gradually wears out, and depreciation is charged each year to spread its cost over its useful life.', 'If a arable farm failed to record depreciation on its tractor, the value shown for that asset in its accounts would be overstated.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense; the cash was paid when the printing press was originally purchased, not when the annual depreciation charge is recorded.', 'TRUE — The straight-line method charges an equal amount of depreciation on printing press in every year of its useful life, so book value falls steadily.', 'FALSE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense, and a business''s cash flow for the year will not usually equal its reported profit.', 'TRUE — Fixed assets like tractor lose value through use, and depreciation allocates their cost across the years they are expected to be used.', 'TRUE — Without depreciation, an asset such as tractor would remain at its original cost in the accounts even though it has lost value through use.'], '3/5', 50, 'full' ),
( '6.2', 'CASE 6.2.051', 'Chart: Equity Versus Total Assets', 'Consider the following two-year statement of profit and loss (in € thousands) for a business whose identity is not disclosed.

