Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['On total assets of €1207 thousand, the current ratio exceeds 1.20.', 'Working capital of €121 thousand is positive on this balance sheet.', 'The equity ratio on assets of €1207 thousand is below 30%.', 'Buildings of €462 thousand exceed machinery of €247 thousand.', 'Cash of €115 thousand exceeds trade receivables of €81 thousand.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Current ratio is about 1.42.', 'TRUE — Working capital equals 121.', 'FALSE — Equity ratio is about 48.8%.', 'TRUE — Buildings 462 vs machinery 247.', 'TRUE — Cash 115 vs receivables 81.'], '4/5', 125, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.001', 'Reading Financial Statements Cautiously in Practice', 'Analyze how a single year''s balance sheet or income statement can be misleading without wider context. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A single year''s balance sheet or income statement rarely tells the full story about a business, so the figures are best interpreted alongside additional context rather than read in isolation.', 'Because published financial statements condense complex operations into a handful of line items, readers should interpret the figures cautiously rather than draw firm conclusions from any single number.', 'A single year''s balance sheet or income statement always tells the full story about a business, so no other years or comparisons are ever needed.', 'A rise in profit for the year does not by itself prove that a business is being managed well, since the improvement could stem from a one-off item rather than from the core operations.', 'Because financial statements present exact figures, readers never need to interpret them cautiously or look for additional explanation.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Figures from one year alone lack context, so readers should look beyond a single year before drawing conclusions.', 'TRUE — Summarised statements can hide detail, so single figures deserve cautious interpretation.', 'FALSE — A single year''s figures rarely tell the full story; wider context and comparison are needed.', 'TRUE — A profit increase may come from a one-off gain rather than genuinely improved management.', 'FALSE — Summarised figures still require cautious interpretation and often benefit from additional explanation.'], '2/5', 1, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.002', 'Reading Financial Statements Cautiously Explained', 'Analyze why comparing financial statements across several years reveals trends invisible in one year alone. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A rise in profit for the year always proves that a business is being managed well, regardless of whether the improvement stems from a one-off item.', 'Notes accompanying the financial statements are optional extras that never explain movements shown in the main balance sheet and income statement.', 'A single year''s balance sheet and income statement reveal every trend in a business''s performance without needing figures from other years.', 'Notes accompanying the financial statements often explain unusual movements that the main balance sheet and income statement alone do not make clear.', 'Tracking revenue, cost of sales and profit for the year over multiple years adds no useful information beyond what one year''s figures already show.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — A profit rise can stem from a one-off item rather than genuinely improved management.', 'FALSE — Notes often explain unusual movements that the main statements alone do not make clear.', 'FALSE — Trends only become visible when several years of figures are compared, not from one year alone.', 'TRUE — Supporting notes provide context that the main statements alone cannot always convey.', 'FALSE — Multi-year tracking reveals trends that a single year''s figures cannot show.'], '5/5', 2, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.003', 'Reading Financial Statements Cautiously for Analysts', 'Consider a construction contractor whose finance director is benchmarking the company''s balance sheet against those of rival firms in the same region. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Comparing a business''s balance sheet and income statement across several years reveals trends that a single year''s figures cannot show on their own.', 'Tracking revenue, cost of sales and profit for the year over multiple years helps show whether a business''s performance is improving or deteriorating.', 'A year-on-year comparison of the balance sheet can reveal whether a business''s asset base and financing structure are expanding or contracting over time.', 'Reviewing several consecutive years of financial statements helps distinguish a temporary fluctuation from a lasting change in a business''s performance.', 'Comparing a business''s financial statements with those of similar businesses in the same industry provides a benchmark for judging whether its results are strong or weak.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Multi-year comparison exposes trends hidden within any single year''s figures.', 'TRUE — Multi-year tracking of key figures reveals the direction of a business''s performance.', 'TRUE — Comparing balance sheets year on year shows whether assets and financing are growing or shrinking.', 'TRUE — Multiple years of data help separate a one-off blip from a genuine, lasting change.', 'TRUE — Industry peers provide the benchmark needed to judge whether results are strong or weak.'], '4/5', 3, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.004', 'Reading Financial Statements Cautiously Over Time', 'Analyze how the balance between current and non-current assets reflects a business''s capital intensity. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A year-on-year comparison of the balance sheet cannot reveal anything about whether a business''s asset base or financing structure is changing.', 'A profit figure that looks impressive when viewed alone may appear only ordinary once it is compared with the results of similar businesses in the same industry.', 'Industry benchmarks allow analysts to judge whether a business''s asset structure or profitability is typical for its sector rather than exceptional or weak.', 'Reviewing several consecutive years of financial statements makes it harder, not easier, to distinguish a temporary fluctuation from a lasting change.', 'Comparing a business''s financial statements with those of similar businesses in the same industry provides no useful benchmark for judging its results.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Year-on-year balance sheet comparison is exactly how changes in assets and financing are detected.', 'TRUE — Context from comparable businesses can turn an apparently impressive figure into an ordinary one.', 'TRUE — Sector benchmarks show whether results are typical, exceptional or weak for that industry.', 'FALSE — More years of data make it easier, not harder, to separate a blip from a lasting change.', 'FALSE — Industry comparisons are exactly the benchmark used to judge whether results are strong or weak.'], '4/5', 4, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.005', 'Reading Financial Statements Cautiously in Context', 'Analyze how the source of growth in equity, retained earnings versus new share capital, reveals a business''s financing pattern. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A profit figure that looks impressive when viewed alone remains equally impressive once compared with similar businesses in the same industry.', 'Reading a business''s financial statements alongside those of its competitors helps reveal whether its performance reflects the whole industry or is specific to that business.', 'Industry benchmarks are irrelevant to judging whether a business''s asset structure or profitability is typical for its sector.', 'The proportion of a business''s total assets held as non-current assets compared with current assets indicates how capital-intensive its operations are.', 'A business with a high share of current assets relative to non-current assets tends to hold more resources that can be turned into cash within a year.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['FALSE — A seemingly impressive figure can look merely ordinary once compared with industry peers.', 'TRUE — Comparing with competitors clarifies whether a result is industry-wide or company-specific.', 'FALSE — Industry benchmarks are precisely what shows whether results are typical for the sector.', 'TRUE — The non-current versus current asset split signals how capital-intensive a business is.', 'TRUE — A current-asset-heavy structure means more resources are convertible into cash within a year.'], '5/5', 5, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.006', 'Comparing Results Across Years in Practice', 'Analyze why non-current assets are best financed by equity and non-current liabilities rather than short-term borrowing. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Analyzing the split between current and non-current assets on the balance sheet helps explain how a business ties up its resources.', 'A rising share of non-current assets over successive years may indicate that a business is investing more heavily in long-term production capacity.', 'Reading a business''s financial statements alongside its competitors'' statements never helps explain whether its performance is industry-wide or specific to that business.', 'Comparing share capital and retained earnings over time shows whether growth in equity stems from new owner contributions or from the accumulation of prior profits.', 'A business''s financing mix, meaning the balance between equity and the various liabilities on its balance sheet, can be examined by tracking its composition over successive years.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — The current versus non-current split explains how a business''s resources are tied up.', 'TRUE — A growing non-current asset share can signal heavier long-term investment.', 'FALSE — Comparing with competitors helps distinguish industry-wide trends from company-specific results.', 'TRUE — Tracking share capital versus retained earnings reveals the source of equity growth.', 'TRUE — The financing mix of equity and liabilities can be tracked over successive balance sheets.'], '2/5', 6, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.007', 'Comparing Results Across Years Explained', 'Consider a food processing company whose accountant is explaining the difference between cash spent on new equipment and the expense recognised in the income statement. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['An increase in equity that comes mainly from retained earnings rather than new share capital indicates that a business is financing its growth internally.', 'Reviewing how a business''s equity has developed over several years reveals whether it relies more on retained profit or on external owner contributions for growth.', 'The proportion of a business''s total assets held as non-current versus current assets says nothing about how capital-intensive its operations are.', 'A business with a high share of current assets relative to non-current assets tends to hold fewer resources that can be quickly converted into cash.', 'Non-current assets are considered adequately financed when their value is covered by the sum of equity and non-current liabilities.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Equity growth driven by retained earnings rather than new shares reflects internal financing.', 'TRUE — Multi-year equity review shows reliance on retained profit versus external contributions.', 'FALSE — The non-current versus current asset split is a direct indicator of capital intensity.', 'FALSE — A higher share of current assets means more, not fewer, resources convertible into cash quickly.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets are adequately financed when covered by equity plus non-current liabilities.'], '3/5', 7, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.008', 'Comparing Results Across Years for Analysts', 'Analyze why administrative, distribution and selling costs are excluded from cost of sales. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Analyzing the split between current and non-current assets on the balance sheet provides no insight into how a business ties up its resources.', 'Financing non-current assets primarily with long-term sources of capital, rather than short-term borrowing, is regarded as financially sound practice.', 'A rising share of non-current assets over successive years always indicates declining investment in long-term production capacity.', 'Comparing share capital and retained earnings over time can never reveal whether equity growth stems from new owner contributions or accumulated profit.', 'A business''s financing mix, meaning the balance between equity and liabilities, cannot be examined by tracking its balance sheet composition over time.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — The current versus non-current split is precisely what shows how resources are tied up.', 'TRUE — Long-term financing of long-term assets is regarded as sound financial practice.', 'FALSE — A rising non-current asset share typically signals more, not less, long-term investment.', 'FALSE — Comparing share capital and retained earnings is exactly how the source of equity growth is revealed.', 'FALSE — Tracking balance sheet composition over time is precisely how the financing mix is examined.'], '3/5', 8, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.009', 'Comparing Results Across Years Over Time', 'Analyze how gross profit is calculated before operating expenses are deducted from revenue. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['An increase in equity that comes mainly from retained earnings rather than new share capital indicates that new capital has been contributed by the owners.', 'Reviewing how a business''s equity has developed over several years reveals nothing about whether it relies on retained profit or external contributions.', 'If non-current assets exceed the combined total of equity and non-current liabilities, part of those long-term assets is being financed by current liabilities, which is considered risky.', 'Non-current assets are considered adequately financed only when their value is covered entirely by current liabilities.', 'Financing non-current assets primarily with short-term borrowing rather than long-term capital is regarded as the financially sound approach.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — If share capital is unchanged, equity growth must come from retained earnings, not new capital.', 'FALSE — Multi-year equity review is exactly how reliance on retained profit versus external contributions is judged.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets exceeding equity plus non-current liabilities implies risky short-term financing of long-term assets.', 'FALSE — Non-current assets are adequately financed when covered by equity and non-current liabilities, not current liabilities.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets mainly with short-term borrowing is considered risky, not sound.'], '5/5', 9, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.010', 'Comparing Results Across Years in Context', 'Analyze why the operating result, also known as earnings before interest and taxes, isolates core trading performance from financing and tax effects. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Long-term investments such as buildings and machinery are best matched with long-term sources of finance rather than short-term credit that must soon be repaid.', 'Cost of sales comprises the direct costs of producing or acquiring the goods that a business has sold, such as materials and direct production labour.', 'Cost of sales is limited to the direct costs of production, so administrative overheads and selling expenses appear elsewhere in the income statement.', 'The cost of raw materials consumed in manufacturing the goods sold forms part of cost of sales, unlike costs incurred after production is complete.', 'Because cost of sales captures only direct production costs, a business''s administrative and distribution functions are reported separately further down the income statement.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Long-term assets should be matched with long-term finance, not short-term credit.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales is limited to direct production or acquisition costs such as materials and direct labour.', 'TRUE — Administrative and selling expenses sit outside cost of sales, further down the income statement.', 'TRUE — Raw materials consumed in production belong in cost of sales; post-production costs do not.', 'TRUE — Direct-cost-only cost of sales means administration and distribution are reported separately.'], '3/5', 10, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.011', 'Benchmarking Against Competitors in Practice', 'Consider a textile manufacturer whose board is discussing how the split between current and non-current assets has changed since the factory was expanded. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Gross profit is calculated by deducting cost of sales from revenue, before any operating expenses such as distribution or administrative costs are subtracted.', 'A business''s gross profit reflects the margin earned on producing and selling goods before overheads such as administration and distribution are considered.', 'Distribution costs and general administrative costs are deducted after gross profit has already been calculated, on the way to the operating result.', 'Because gross profit is calculated before operating expenses, two businesses with identical gross profit can still end up with very different operating results.', 'The operating result, also referred to as earnings before interest and taxes, measures profit generated from a business''s core operations before financing costs and income tax are taken into account.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Gross profit equals revenue minus cost of sales, ahead of any operating expense deductions.', 'TRUE — Gross profit reflects the production and selling margin before overheads are deducted.', 'TRUE — Distribution and administrative costs are deducted after gross profit, on the way to the operating result.', 'TRUE — Identical gross profit can still lead to different operating results depending on operating expenses.', 'TRUE — The operating result, also called earnings before interest and taxes, excludes financing costs and income tax.'], '3/5', 11, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.012', 'Benchmarking Against Competitors Explained', 'Analyze how the relative growth of revenue and cost of sales over time affects a business''s gross profit margin. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because the operating result excludes finance costs and income tax, it allows the core trading performance of a business to be assessed separately from how it is financed or taxed.', 'Comparing the operating result across several years shows how a business''s core trading performance is developing independently of financing decisions or tax rates.', 'If non-current assets exceed the combined total of equity and non-current liabilities, this is always a sign of prudent financial management.', 'Two businesses with the same operating result can end up with different profit for the year if their finance costs or tax rates differ.', 'An expenditure represents an outflow of cash or resources, whereas an expense represents the portion of that outflow recognised in the income statement as relating to the current period.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Excluding finance costs and tax lets the operating result show core trading performance alone.', 'TRUE — Multi-year operating result comparison isolates trading performance from financing and tax choices.', 'FALSE — Non-current assets exceeding equity plus non-current liabilities signals risky, not prudent, financing.', 'TRUE — Equal operating results can still lead to different profit for the year due to financing or tax differences.', 'TRUE — Expenditure is the outflow itself; expense is the portion recognised for the current period.'], '4/5', 12, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.013', 'Benchmarking Against Competitors for Analysts', 'Review how a single year''s balance sheet or income statement can be misleading without wider context. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Prepaying next year''s insurance premium is an expenditure in the current year, but it only becomes an expense once the coverage period it relates to has passed.', 'Distinguishing expenditure from expense matters because a large cash outflow in one year can be spread across several years'' worth of expense recognition.', 'A business can make a substantial expenditure in a given year while recognising only a small portion of it as an expense in that year''s income statement.', 'Tracking revenue over several years shows whether a business''s sales are growing, stagnating or declining.', 'A business whose revenue rises every year is not necessarily improving profitability, since its costs could be rising even faster.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — A prepayment is an expenditure now but becomes an expense only in the period it covers.', 'TRUE — One large expenditure can translate into expense recognised over several future years.', 'TRUE — A large expenditure need not translate into a large expense in the same year.', 'TRUE — Multi-year revenue tracking reveals whether sales are growing, stagnating or declining.', 'TRUE — Rising revenue alone does not guarantee improving profitability if costs rise faster still.'], '2/5', 13, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.014', 'Benchmarking Against Competitors Over Time', 'Review why comparing financial statements across several years reveals trends invisible in one year alone. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Consistent revenue growth over multiple years is a more reliable signal of business health than a single year''s strong sales figure.', 'A sudden jump in revenue in one year deserves closer examination before assuming that a business''s underlying demand has permanently improved.', 'Long-term investments such as buildings and machinery are best matched with short-term credit that must soon be repaid.', 'Comparing the growth rate of revenue with the growth rate of cost of sales over successive years shows whether a business is becoming more or less efficient at producing what it sells.', 'A business whose cost of sales rises broadly in line with its revenue is likely to maintain a stable gross profit margin from one year to the next.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Sustained multi-year growth is a more reliable health signal than one strong year.', 'TRUE — A one-year revenue jump warrants scrutiny before assuming a lasting demand improvement.', 'FALSE — Long-term assets are best matched with long-term finance, not short-term credit.', 'TRUE — Comparing revenue growth with cost of sales growth reveals changing production efficiency.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales rising in step with revenue tends to keep the gross profit margin stable.'], '5/5', 14, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.015', 'Benchmarking Against Competitors in Context', 'Consider a wholesale hardware supplier whose accountant is walking new staff through the difference between cost of sales and the operating expenses reported below it. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If revenue grows while cost of sales grows more slowly, a business''s gross profit margin will widen over that period.', 'Examining how revenue and cost of sales move together over time helps explain changes in a business''s gross profit margin.', 'A furniture manufacturer is regarded as financing its factory buildings soundly when their value is covered by equity together with a long-term bank loan.', 'Tracking the proportion of factory buildings against inventory on a furniture manufacturer''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'If a furniture manufacturer''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Revenue outpacing cost of sales growth widens the gross profit margin.', 'TRUE — Joint examination of revenue and cost of sales trends explains gross profit margin changes.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.'], '2/5', 15, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.016', 'The Current and Non-Current Asset Mix in Practice', 'Review how the balance between current and non-current assets reflects a business''s capital intensity. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Cost of sales includes every cost a business incurs, from production through to administration and delivery.', 'Cost of sales covers administrative overheads and selling expenses in addition to the direct costs of production.', 'A supermarket chain is regarded as financing its production machinery soundly when their value is covered by equity together with bonds payable.', 'The cost of raw materials consumed in manufacturing the goods sold is excluded from cost of sales because it is a production cost.', 'Tracking the proportion of production machinery against trade receivables on a supermarket chain''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — Cost of sales is limited to direct production costs; administration and delivery sit elsewhere.', 'FALSE — Administrative overheads and selling expenses are excluded from cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'FALSE — Raw materials consumed in production are a direct cost and belong within cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.'], '5/5', 16, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.017', 'The Current and Non-Current Asset Mix Explained', 'Review how the source of growth in equity, retained earnings versus new share capital, reveals a business''s financing pattern. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Because cost of sales captures every departmental cost, a business''s administrative and distribution functions are reported within it rather than separately.', 'If a supermarket chain''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'A construction contractor is regarded as financing its office equipment soundly when their value is covered by equity together with a long-term lease liability.', 'Gross profit is calculated by deducting all operating expenses, including distribution and administrative costs, from revenue.', 'A business''s gross profit reflects overall profitability after administration and distribution overheads have already been deducted.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Administrative and distribution costs are reported separately from cost of sales, not within it.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'FALSE — Gross profit only deducts cost of sales from revenue; operating expenses are deducted later.', 'FALSE — Gross profit is calculated before, not after, administration and distribution overheads.'], '4/5', 17, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.018', 'The Current and Non-Current Asset Mix for Analysts', 'Review why non-current assets are best financed by equity and non-current liabilities rather than short-term borrowing. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Tracking the proportion of office equipment against cash and cash equivalents on a construction contractor''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'If a construction contractor''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'A freight and logistics company is regarded as financing its patents, trademarks and licences soundly when their value is covered by equity together with a long-term mortgage.', 'Distribution costs and general administrative costs are deducted before gross profit is calculated, alongside cost of sales.', 'Tracking the proportion of patents, trademarks and licences against prepaid expenses on a freight and logistics company''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'FALSE — Distribution and administrative costs are deducted after gross profit, not alongside cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.'], '2/5', 18, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.019', 'The Current and Non-Current Asset Mix Over Time', 'Consider a manufacturing group whose management accountants are tracing how an expenditure on new machinery will be expensed gradually over its useful life. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a freight and logistics company''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'Because gross profit already reflects operating expenses, two businesses with identical gross profit must always report identical operating results.', 'The operating result, also referred to as earnings before interest and taxes, is calculated after deducting finance costs and income tax.', 'A specialist engineering firm is regarded as financing its delivery vehicles soundly when their value is covered by equity together with long-term borrowings.', 'Tracking the proportion of delivery vehicles against short-term investments on a specialist engineering firm''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'FALSE — Identical gross profit can still lead to different operating results depending on operating expenses.', 'FALSE — The operating result is calculated before, not after, finance costs and income tax.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.'], '5/5', 19, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.020', 'The Current and Non-Current Asset Mix in Context', 'Review why administrative, distribution and selling costs are excluded from cost of sales. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a specialist engineering firm''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'A hotel group is regarded as financing its computer hardware soundly when their value is covered by equity together with long-term supplier financing.', 'Tracking the proportion of computer hardware against raw material stocks on a hotel group''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'Because the operating result includes finance costs and income tax, it cannot be used to assess a business''s core trading performance separately from financing.', 'A food processing company is regarded as financing its leasehold premises soundly when their value is covered by equity together with deferred tax liabilities.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'FALSE — The operating result excludes finance costs and tax, which is exactly why it isolates core trading performance.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.'], '2/5', 20, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.021', 'Sources of Equity Growth in Practice', 'Review how gross profit is calculated before operating expenses are deducted from revenue. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Tracking the proportion of leasehold premises against finished goods stock on a food processing company''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'If a food processing company''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'Comparing the operating result across several years reveals nothing about a business''s core trading performance because it already reflects financing and tax decisions.', 'An electronics retailer is regarded as financing its specialist production plant soundly when their value is covered by equity together with long-term pension obligations.', 'Tracking the proportion of specialist production plant against accrued income on an electronics retailer''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'FALSE — The operating result excludes financing and tax decisions, so its trend reflects core trading performance.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.'], '5/5', 21, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.022', 'Sources of Equity Growth Explained', 'Review why the operating result, also known as earnings before interest and taxes, isolates core trading performance from financing and tax effects. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Two businesses with the same operating result must always report the same profit for the year regardless of their finance costs or tax rates.', 'If an electronics retailer''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'An expenditure and an expense always occur in the same accounting period and therefore mean exactly the same thing.', 'Prepaying next year''s insurance premium is recognised as a full expense in the income statement of the year it is paid.', 'Distinguishing expenditure from expense is unnecessary because every cash outflow is automatically an expense in the period it occurs.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Differing finance costs or tax rates can still produce different profit for the year from the same operating result.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'FALSE — Expenditure is a cash outflow while expense is the portion recognised in a period; they can differ in timing.', 'FALSE — A prepayment becomes an expense only in the period the coverage relates to, not when paid.', 'FALSE — Not every cash outflow is an expense in the period it occurs; some are spread over future periods.'], '5/5', 22, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.023', 'Sources of Equity Growth for Analysts', 'Consider a freight and logistics company whose new chief financial officer is examining how the balance sheet has evolved since the business last expanded its vehicle fleet. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A printing business is regarded as financing its warehouse facilities soundly when their value is covered by equity together with a long-term equipment loan.', 'A business that makes a substantial expenditure in a given year must recognise the entire amount as an expense in that same year.', 'Tracking the proportion of warehouse facilities against supplies on hand on a printing business''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'Tracking revenue over several years provides no indication of whether a business''s sales are growing, stagnating or declining.', 'If a printing business''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'FALSE — A large expenditure can be recognised as expense gradually over several future years.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'FALSE — Multi-year revenue tracking is exactly how growth, stagnation or decline in sales is identified.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.'], '5/5', 23, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.024', 'Sources of Equity Growth Over Time', 'Review how the relative growth of revenue and cost of sales over time affects a business''s gross profit margin. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A pharmaceutical distributor is regarded as financing its manufacturing tooling soundly when their value is covered by equity together with long-term debentures.', 'Tracking the proportion of manufacturing tooling against marketable securities on a pharmaceutical distributor''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'A business whose revenue rises every year is automatically improving its profitability, regardless of how its costs are behaving.', 'A single year''s strong sales figure is a more reliable signal of business health than consistent revenue growth over multiple years.', 'If a pharmaceutical distributor''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'FALSE — Rising revenue does not guarantee improving profitability if costs are rising even faster.', 'FALSE — Sustained multi-year growth is a more reliable signal than one strong year alone.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.'], '4/5', 24, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.025', 'Sources of Equity Growth in Context', 'Examine how a single year''s balance sheet or income statement can be misleading without wider context. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A textile manufacturer is regarded as financing its long-term equipment investments soundly when their value is covered by equity together with a long-term construction loan.', 'Tracking the proportion of long-term equipment investments against work in progress on a textile manufacturer''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'If a textile manufacturer''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'A vehicle repair chain is regarded as financing its company-owned land soundly when their value is covered by equity together with long-term finance lease obligations.', 'Tracking the proportion of company-owned land against advance payments to suppliers on a vehicle repair chain''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.'], '5/5', 25, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.026', 'Financing Mix and Capital Structure in Practice', 'Examine why comparing financial statements across several years reveals trends invisible in one year alone. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A sudden jump in revenue in one year should always be assumed to reflect a permanent improvement in underlying demand.', 'If a vehicle repair chain''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'An agricultural cooperative is regarded as financing its processing plant soundly when their value is covered by equity together with a long-term shareholder loan.', 'Tracking the proportion of processing plant against short-term deposits on an agricultural cooperative''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'A software services company is regarded as financing its distribution centre buildings soundly when their value is covered by equity together with long-term bank financing.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['FALSE — A one-year revenue jump warrants closer examination rather than an assumption of permanence.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.'], '3/5', 26, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.027', 'Financing Mix and Capital Structure Explained', 'Consider an electronics retailer whose management team is analysing whether recent growth in equity has come from retained profit or from new share issues. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Tracking the proportion of distribution centre buildings against spare parts inventory on a software services company''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'Comparing the growth rate of revenue with the growth rate of cost of sales over successive years reveals nothing about production efficiency.', 'A business whose cost of sales rises broadly in line with its revenue will typically see its gross profit margin swing wildly from year to year.', 'If a software services company''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'If revenue grows while cost of sales grows more slowly, a business''s gross profit margin will narrow over that period.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'FALSE — Comparing revenue and cost of sales growth rates is exactly how changes in production efficiency are revealed.', 'FALSE — Cost of sales rising in line with revenue tends to keep the gross profit margin stable, not volatile.', 'TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'FALSE — Revenue outpacing cost of sales growth widens, rather than narrows, the gross profit margin.'], '5/5', 27, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.028', 'Financing Mix and Capital Structure for Analysts', 'Examine how the balance between current and non-current assets reflects a business''s capital intensity. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Examining how revenue and cost of sales move together over time provides no explanation for changes in a business''s gross profit margin.', 'A furniture manufacturer is considered financially sound when its factory buildings are financed mainly through trade payables, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'A wholesale hardware supplier is regarded as financing its capitalised development costs soundly when their value is covered by equity together with a long-term infrastructure loan.', 'Tracking the proportion of capitalised development costs against outstanding customer invoices on a wholesale hardware supplier''s balance sheet over several years shows whether the business is becoming more or less capital-intensive.', 'The mix of factory buildings and inventory on a furniture manufacturer''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Joint examination of revenue and cost of sales trends is exactly what explains gross profit margin changes.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'TRUE — Non-current assets covered by equity and non-current liabilities reflect sound long-term financing.', 'TRUE — Changes in the non-current versus current asset split over time signal shifting capital intensity.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.'], '5/5', 28, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.029', 'Financing Mix and Capital Structure Over Time', 'Examine how the source of growth in equity, retained earnings versus new share capital, reveals a business''s financing pattern. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a wholesale hardware supplier''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity has come from internally generated profit rather than new owner investment.', 'If a furniture manufacturer''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'A supermarket chain is considered financially sound when its production machinery are financed mainly through a bank overdraft, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of production machinery and trade receivables on a supermarket chain''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'The pay of administrative staff is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — With share capital unchanged, rising equity must stem from retained earnings, i.e. internal financing.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.'], '5/5', 29, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.030', 'Financing Mix and Capital Structure in Context', 'Examine why non-current assets are best financed by equity and non-current liabilities rather than short-term borrowing. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Wages earned by delivery drivers are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'Compensation paid to sales representatives is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'Raw materials consumed in production are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'For a furniture manufacturer, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.', 'The pay of office administrators is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.'], '5/5', 30, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.031', 'Matching Non-Current Assets to Long-Term Finance in Practice', 'Consider a vehicle repair chain whose finance team is separating administrative, delivery and sales costs from the direct costs of servicing customer vehicles. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a supermarket chain''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'Wages earned by warehouse dispatch staff are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'A construction contractor is considered financially sound when its office equipment are financed mainly through accrued wages, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of office equipment and cash and cash equivalents on a construction contractor''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'Compensation paid to sales commission staff is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.'], '4/5', 31, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.032', 'Matching Non-Current Assets to Long-Term Finance Explained', 'Examine why administrative, distribution and selling costs are excluded from cost of sales. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Direct production labour are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'If a construction contractor''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'For a supermarket chain, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.', 'The pay of human resources personnel is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.', 'A freight and logistics company is considered financially sound when its patents, trademarks and licences are financed mainly through short-term loans, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.'], '5/5', 32, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.033', 'Matching Non-Current Assets to Long-Term Finance for Analysts', 'Examine how gross profit is calculated before operating expenses are deducted from revenue. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Wages earned by shipping coordinators are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'The mix of patents, trademarks and licences and prepaid expenses on a freight and logistics company''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'If a freight and logistics company''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'A specialist engineering firm is considered financially sound when its delivery vehicles are financed mainly through taxes payable, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of delivery vehicles and short-term investments on a specialist engineering firm''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.'], '5/5', 33, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.034', 'Matching Non-Current Assets to Long-Term Finance Over Time', 'Examine why the operating result, also known as earnings before interest and taxes, isolates core trading performance from financing and tax effects. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a specialist engineering firm''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'Compensation paid to the sales team is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'A hotel group is considered financially sound when its computer hardware are financed mainly through unearned revenue, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of computer hardware and raw material stocks on a hotel group''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'A food processing company is considered financially sound when its leasehold premises are financed mainly through accrued interest, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.'], '5/5', 34, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.035', 'Matching Non-Current Assets to Long-Term Finance in Context', 'Consider a bakery chain whose owners are comparing this year''s balance sheet with last year''s to judge whether the business''s financing structure has become more or less risky. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Components purchased for assembly are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'For a construction contractor, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.', 'The pay of accounting clerks is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.', 'Wages earned by outbound logistics personnel are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'The mix of leasehold premises and finished goods stock on a food processing company''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.', 'TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.'], '4/5', 35, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.036', 'Defining Cost of Sales in Practice', 'Examine how the relative growth of revenue and cost of sales over time affects a business''s gross profit margin. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If a food processing company''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'An electronics retailer is considered financially sound when its specialist production plant are financed mainly through the current portion of long-term debt, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of specialist production plant and accrued income on an electronics retailer''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'Compensation paid to account managers is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'A printing business is considered financially sound when its warehouse facilities are financed mainly through dividends payable, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.'], '2/5', 36, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.037', 'Defining Cost of Sales Explained', 'Assess how a single year''s balance sheet or income statement can be misleading without wider context. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The mix of warehouse facilities and supplies on hand on a printing business''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'Manufacturing overhead directly tied to output are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'For a freight and logistics company, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.', 'If a printing business''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'The pay of finance department employees is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.'], '3/5', 37, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.038', 'Defining Cost of Sales for Analysts', 'Assess why comparing financial statements across several years reveals trends invisible in one year alone. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A pharmaceutical distributor is considered financially sound when its manufacturing tooling are financed mainly through short-term supplier credit, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of manufacturing tooling and marketable securities on a pharmaceutical distributor''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'Wages earned by freight and courier staff are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'Compensation paid to sales staff is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'If a pharmaceutical distributor''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.'], '4/5', 38, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.039', 'Defining Cost of Sales Over Time', 'Consider a family-run furniture workshop reviewing its newly prepared balance sheet and income statement before meeting its bank manager. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Materials used to manufacture the goods sold are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'A textile manufacturer is considered financially sound when its long-term equipment investments are financed mainly through accrued utility bills, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of long-term equipment investments and work in progress on a textile manufacturer''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'For a specialist engineering firm, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.', 'The pay of reception and clerical staff is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.'], '3/5', 39, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.040', 'Defining Cost of Sales in Context', 'Assess how the balance between current and non-current assets reflects a business''s capital intensity. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Wages earned by distribution centre workers are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'Compensation paid to the retail sales force is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'Direct labour on the factory floor are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'If a textile manufacturer''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'For a hotel group, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.'], '5/5', 40, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.041', 'Excluding Overheads from Cost of Sales in Practice', 'Assess how the source of growth in equity, retained earnings versus new share capital, reveals a business''s financing pattern. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The pay of management support staff is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.', 'Wages earned by despatch clerks are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'Compensation paid to business development staff is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'Production supplies consumed making the goods sold are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'For a food processing company, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.', 'TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.'], '5/5', 41, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.042', 'Excluding Overheads from Cost of Sales Explained', 'Assess why non-current assets are best financed by equity and non-current liabilities rather than short-term borrowing. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The pay of back-office employees is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.', 'A vehicle repair chain is considered financially sound when its company-owned land are financed mainly through short-term borrowings, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of company-owned land and advance payments to suppliers on a vehicle repair chain''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'If a vehicle repair chain''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'An agricultural cooperative is considered financially sound when its processing plant are financed mainly through outstanding supplier invoices, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.'], '5/5', 42, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.043', 'Excluding Overheads from Cost of Sales for Analysts', 'Consider a specialist engineering firm preparing a presentation that compares its cost of sales and gross profit trends over the past five years. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Wages earned by outbound delivery staff are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'Compensation paid to sales department employees is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'The purchase cost of goods bought for resale are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'For an electronics retailer, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.', 'The pay of payroll administrators is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.'], '5/5', 43, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.044', 'Excluding Overheads from Cost of Sales Over Time', 'Assess why administrative, distribution and selling costs are excluded from cost of sales. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Wages earned by delivery fleet employees are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'The mix of processing plant and short-term deposits on an agricultural cooperative''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'If an agricultural cooperative''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'A software services company is considered financially sound when its distribution centre buildings are financed mainly through a short-term line of credit, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'The mix of distribution centre buildings and spare parts inventory on a software services company''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.'], '5/5', 44, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.045', 'Excluding Overheads from Cost of Sales in Context', 'Assess how gross profit is calculated before operating expenses are deducted from revenue. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Compensation paid to customer-facing sales staff is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'If a software services company''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.', 'Direct costs of the manufacturing process are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'For a printing business, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.', 'The pay of general office staff is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.'], '5/5', 45, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.046', 'Calculating Gross Profit in Practice', 'Assess why the operating result, also known as earnings before interest and taxes, isolates core trading performance from financing and tax effects. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A wholesale hardware supplier is considered financially sound when its capitalised development costs are financed mainly through accrued operating costs, since short-term liabilities are cheaper to arrange.', 'Wages earned by packing and shipping staff are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'Compensation paid to the commercial sales team is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'The mix of capitalised development costs and outstanding customer invoices on a wholesale hardware supplier''s balance sheet has no bearing on how capital-intensive the business is.', 'If a wholesale hardware supplier''s retained earnings increase while share capital stays unchanged, the resulting rise in equity must have come from new shares issued to the owners.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Financing long-term assets with current liabilities is risky, since short-term liabilities must soon be repaid or renewed.', 'TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'FALSE — The asset mix directly reflects capital intensity: more non-current assets means a more capital-intensive business.', 'FALSE — With share capital unchanged, equity growth cannot come from new shares; it must stem from retained earnings.'], '5/5', 46, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.047', 'Calculating Gross Profit Explained', 'Consider a printing business whose owner is reviewing the balance sheet to judge whether long-term assets are being financed appropriately. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Costs directly incurred to produce the units sold are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'For a pharmaceutical distributor, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.', 'The pay of head office administrators is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.', 'Wages earned by transport and delivery staff are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'Compensation paid to field sales agents is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.', 'TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.'], '4/5', 47, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.048', 'Calculating Gross Profit for Analysts', 'Assess how the relative growth of revenue and cost of sales over time affects a business''s gross profit margin. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The pay of administrative staff is included within cost of sales because every employee ultimately contributes to a business''s output.', 'Wages earned by delivery drivers are included within cost of sales because delivering the product is considered part of production.', 'Direct materials used in the goods sold are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.', 'Compensation paid to sales representatives is included within cost of sales because selling is necessary to generate revenue.', 'For a textile manufacturer, cost of sales reflects only the direct costs of producing what is sold, while administrative and selling costs sit further down the income statement.'], ARRAY[false, false, true, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative pay is excluded regardless of overall contribution.', 'FALSE — Delivery activity happens after production and is a distribution cost, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.', 'FALSE — Selling costs are operating expenses incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative and selling costs appear separately.'], '5/5', 48, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.049', 'Calculating Gross Profit Over Time', 'Consider how a single year''s balance sheet or income statement can be misleading without wider context. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Raw materials consumed in production are excluded from cost of sales because only cash payments count as production costs.', 'The pay of corporate support staff is excluded from cost of sales because their work does not directly relate to producing the goods that are sold.', 'For a furniture manufacturer, cost of sales includes administrative and selling costs because all costs eventually relate to sales.', 'The pay of office administrators is included within cost of sales because every employee ultimately contributes to a business''s output.', 'Wages earned by warehouse dispatch staff are included within cost of sales because delivering the product is considered part of production.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Cost of sales includes all direct production costs regardless of the timing of any related cash payment.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales reflects direct production costs only, not general administrative pay.', 'FALSE — Administrative and selling costs are excluded from cost of sales and reported separately.', 'FALSE — Only direct production costs belong in cost of sales; administrative pay is excluded regardless of overall contribution.', 'FALSE — Delivery activity happens after production and is a distribution cost, not cost of sales.'], '2/5', 49, 'full' ),
( '6.3', 'CASE 6.3.050', 'Calculating Gross Profit in Context', 'Consider why comparing financial statements across several years reveals trends invisible in one year alone. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Compensation paid to sales commission staff is included within cost of sales because selling is necessary to generate revenue.', 'Wages earned by loading dock workers are treated as a distribution cost rather than cost of sales, since they relate to delivering goods after production is complete.', 'Compensation paid to showroom sales staff is kept out of cost of sales because selling activity takes place after the goods have already been produced.', 'Direct production labour are excluded from cost of sales because only cash payments count as production costs.', 'Factory floor wages tied directly to output are included within cost of sales because they are directly incurred in producing the goods a business sells.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — Selling costs are operating expenses incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Delivery and shipping wages are distribution costs, incurred after production, not cost of sales.', 'TRUE — Selling costs are separate from production costs and excluded from cost of sales.', 'FALSE — Cost of sales includes all direct production costs regardless of the timing of any related cash payment.', 'TRUE — Cost of sales captures the direct costs of production, such as materials and direct labour.'], '3/5', 50, 'full' ),
