( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.001', 'Financial Accounting and External Users', 'Review how financial accounting information is prepared primarily for external users such as owners, lenders and tax authorities. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Published financial statements prepared under financial accounting are aimed primarily at tax authorities and other parties outside day-to-day management.', 'Tax authorities typically rely on audited annual reports rather than on informal internal spreadsheets when judging a business.', 'Detailed cost schedules prepared for store managers are examples of management accounting information used inside the business.', 'Store managers may receive weekly or monthly internal reports that are not part of the legally prescribed annual financial statements.', 'A manufacturer may use management accounting to decide whether a product line still covers its variable costs even when published accounts show overall profit.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Financial accounting serves external stakeholders such as tax authorities, not only internal managers.', 'TRUE — External parties such as tax authorities depend on formal published and often audited accounts.', 'TRUE — Management accounting supplies internal decision makers such as store managers with tailored cost and performance data.', 'TRUE — Management accounting can be produced frequently for store managers without following the statutory formats used externally.', 'TRUE — Discontinue-or-continue product decisions for a manufacturer often draw on internal contribution analysis from management accounting.'], '3/5', 1, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.002', 'Management Accounting for Internal Decisions', 'Consider a retail chain whose finance team prepares internal margin reports weekly while lenders review its statutory balance sheet. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Management accounting reports for a manufacturer must be filed in exactly the same statutory format as its published balance sheet and income statement.', 'Managers at a manufacturer can use internal accounting data to review whether marketing expenditure should be reduced in a weak quarter.', 'Only financial accounting can ever support a manufacturer''s decision on whether to discontinue an unprofitable product line.', 'A manufacturer prepares management accounting solely for tax authorities and never for its own managers.', 'Owners of a manufacturer may judge whether retained capital is justified by comparing profit with the risk they bear.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — Management accounting is not bound to the same legal presentation rules as published financial statements.', 'TRUE — Cost-cutting choices at a manufacturer are typical management accounting uses rather than purposes of external reporting.', 'FALSE — Management accounting contribution analysis is commonly used for discontinue-or-continue product decisions.', 'FALSE — Management accounting primarily serves internal managers; tax authorities rely on financial accounting filings.', 'TRUE — Return relative to risk is a central owner concern when reading accounts of a manufacturer.'], '5/5', 2, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.003', 'Owners, Managers and Employees as Users', 'Review the distinction between users inside a business and users outside it when accounts are prepared. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['External lenders of a manufacturer typically base their credit decisions only on informal internal spreadsheets prepared for supervisors.', 'An independent audit of a manufacturer''s annual report is intended to increase confidence that the figures are reliable for external users.', 'Depreciation charged in a manufacturer''s income statement reduces reported profit without causing a cash payment in the same period.', 'Management accounting reports for a manufacturer may be structured by product, region or cost centre rather than mirroring the published financial statements line by line.', 'An audit of a manufacturer''s accounts is performed by the firm''s own sales managers to verify daily cash takings.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Lenders generally rely on published and often audited financial statements rather than internal management packs.', 'TRUE — Auditing provides independent verification sought by outsiders reviewing a manufacturer.', 'TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense in the published accounts of a manufacturer.', 'TRUE — Internal formats for a manufacturer can differ from statutory presentation because management accounting serves different questions.', 'FALSE — Auditing is carried out by an independent firm to test the reliability of published accounts, not by sales managers.'], '4/5', 3, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.004', 'Suppliers Reading Customer Accounts', 'Analyze why owners may care about return on their investment relative to the risk they bear. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A retailer may use management accounting to decide whether a product line still covers its variable costs even when published accounts show overall profit.', 'Managers at a retailer can use internal accounting data to review whether marketing expenditure should be reduced in a weak quarter.', 'Owners of a retailer may judge whether retained capital is justified by comparing profit with the risk they bear.', 'An independent audit of a retailer''s annual report is intended to increase confidence that the figures are reliable for external users.', 'Depreciation charged in a retailer''s income statement reduces reported profit without causing a cash payment in the same period.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Discontinue-or-continue product decisions for a retailer often draw on internal contribution analysis from management accounting.', 'TRUE — Cost-cutting choices at a retailer are typical management accounting uses rather than purposes of external reporting.', 'TRUE — Return relative to risk is a central owner concern when reading accounts of a retailer.', 'TRUE — Auditing provides independent verification sought by outsiders reviewing a retailer.', 'TRUE — Depreciation is a non-cash expense in the published accounts of a retailer.'], '4/5', 4, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.005', 'Investors and Published Profit', 'Review how managers use accounting data to decide whether to cut costs in a particular department. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Depreciation recorded by a manufacturer requires an equal cash payment to leave the business in the same month it is charged.', 'Management accounting reports for a retailer may be structured by product, region or cost centre rather than mirroring the published financial statements line by line.', 'A service firm may use management accounting to decide whether a product line still covers its variable costs even when published accounts show overall profit.', 'Published financial statements of a manufacturer are prepared mainly for shift supervisors who need tomorrow''s production schedule.', 'Owners of a manufacturer never compare profit with the risk of their investment when reading accounts.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, false, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Depreciation reduces profit but does not by itself cause a cash payment when recorded.', 'TRUE — Internal formats for a retailer can differ from statutory presentation because management accounting serves different questions.', 'TRUE — Discontinue-or-continue product decisions for a service firm often draw on internal contribution analysis from management accounting.', 'FALSE — Published financial statements serve external users and overall accountability; daily production scheduling uses internal management information.', 'FALSE — Owners commonly weigh return against risk when judging whether capital should remain in the business.'], '3/5', 5, 'full' ),
