( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.042', 'Shared Cost Allocation Internally', 'Consider a software company whose product managers review contribution figures while investors study its published income statement. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A manufacturer may prepare weekly internal performance summaries for managers even though its statutory financial statements are published only once a year.', 'External regulators do not require a manufacturer to file weekly management accounting packs in the same format as its annual balance sheet.', 'It is accurate to say that for a manufacturer, tax returns are prepared only from management accounting and never from financial accounting records.', 'Pension funds reviewing a service firm typically depend on published financial accounting statements rather than confidential management packs.', 'It is accurate to say that for a manufacturer, auditing replaces the need for any bookkeeping at all.'], ARRAY[false, false, false, true, false], ARRAY['FALSE — Management accounting at a manufacturer can be produced weekly, unlike the annual external filing cycle.', 'FALSE — Weekly internal reports for a manufacturer are not statutory external filings.', 'FALSE — That claim about a manufacturer is incorrect and reverses the usual roles of financial and management accounting.', 'TRUE — External groups such as pension funds use formal published accounts of a service firm, not internal management schedules.', 'FALSE — That claim about a manufacturer is incorrect and reverses the usual roles of financial and management accounting.'], '5/5', 42, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.043', 'Customer Contract Profitability', 'Review how management accounting can highlight which customer contracts are barely covering their variable costs. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Pension funds reviewing a construction contractor typically depend on published financial accounting statements rather than confidential management packs.', 'Trade unions reviewing a manufacturer typically depend on published financial accounting statements rather than confidential management packs.', 'It is accurate to say that for a manufacturer, owners never read published profit figures.', 'Franchise applicants reviewing a service firm typically depend on published financial accounting statements rather than confidential management packs.', 'When a manufacturer focuses on pricing a new catalogue line, the relevant figures are normally drawn from management accounting rather than from the published annual report alone.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — External groups such as pension funds use formal published accounts of a construction contractor, not internal management schedules.', 'TRUE — External groups such as trade unions use formal published accounts of a manufacturer, not internal management schedules.', 'FALSE — That claim about a manufacturer is incorrect and reverses the usual roles of financial and management accounting.', 'TRUE — External groups such as franchise applicants use formal published accounts of a service firm, not internal management schedules.', 'TRUE — Pricing a new catalogue line is a typical internal management accounting use for a manufacturer.'], '2/5', 43, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.044', 'Insufficient Detail in Published Accounts', 'Analyze why financial accounting emphasises the overall financial position rather than individual product margins. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['It is accurate to say that for a manufacturer, suppliers always ignore a customer''s balance sheet before offering credit.', 'Published financial statements of a manufacturer are too aggregated to guide day-to-day choices about pricing a new catalogue line without supplementary internal reports.', 'When a retailer focuses on allocating head-office costs to branches, the relevant figures are normally drawn from management accounting rather than from the published annual report alone.', 'It is accurate to say that for a manufacturer, management accounting must be signed by the same independent auditor as the annual report.', 'Published financial statements of a retailer are too aggregated to guide day-to-day choices about allocating head-office costs to branches without supplementary internal reports.'], ARRAY[false, true, true, false, true], ARRAY['FALSE — That claim about a manufacturer is incorrect and reverses the usual roles of financial and management accounting.', 'TRUE — Internal detail on pricing a new catalogue line goes beyond what external financial accounting provides for a manufacturer.', 'TRUE — Allocating head-office costs to branches is a typical internal management accounting use for a retailer.', 'FALSE — That claim about a manufacturer is incorrect and reverses the usual roles of financial and management accounting.', 'TRUE — Internal detail on allocating head-office costs to branches goes beyond what external financial accounting provides for a retailer.'], '4/5', 44, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.045', 'Listed Company Comparability', 'Review how strategic pricing decisions often combine market information with internal cost data. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['When a service firm focuses on tracking overtime in a single warehouse, the relevant figures are normally drawn from management accounting rather than from the published annual report alone.', 'It is accurate to say that for a manufacturer, financial accounting is prepared exclusively for shop-floor staff.', 'Published financial statements of a service firm are too aggregated to guide day-to-day choices about tracking overtime in a single warehouse without supplementary internal reports.', 'When a construction contractor focuses on comparing margin on two distribution channels, the relevant figures are normally drawn from management accounting rather than from the published annual report alone.', 'Published financial statements of a construction contractor are too aggregated to guide day-to-day choices about comparing margin on two distribution channels without supplementary internal reports.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Tracking overtime in a single warehouse is a typical internal management accounting use for a service firm.', 'FALSE — That claim about a manufacturer is incorrect and reverses the usual roles of financial and management accounting.', 'TRUE — Internal detail on tracking overtime in a single warehouse goes beyond what external financial accounting provides for a service firm.', 'TRUE — Comparing margin on two distribution channels is a typical internal management accounting use for a construction contractor.', 'TRUE — Internal detail on comparing margin on two distribution channels goes beyond what external financial accounting provides for a construction contractor.'], '5/5', 45, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.046', 'Tax Planning Starting From Accounts', 'Consider an engineering consultancy whose partners decide pricing using internal job-cost records while clients never see those internal schedules. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['When a hospitality group focuses on estimating break-even volume for a seasonal product, the relevant figures are normally drawn from management accounting rather than from the published annual report alone.', 'Published financial statements of a hospitality group are too aggregated to guide day-to-day choices about estimating break-even volume for a seasonal product without supplementary internal reports.', 'When a logistics operator focuses on reviewing scrap rates on a production line, the relevant figures are normally drawn from management accounting rather than from the published annual report alone.', 'Published financial statements of a logistics operator are too aggregated to guide day-to-day choices about reviewing scrap rates on a production line without supplementary internal reports.', 'When a health-care provider focuses on monitoring fuel spend across a vehicle fleet, the relevant figures are normally drawn from management accounting rather than from the published annual report alone.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Estimating break-even volume for a seasonal product is a typical internal management accounting use for a hospitality group.', 'TRUE — Internal detail on estimating break-even volume for a seasonal product goes beyond what external financial accounting provides for a hospitality group.', 'TRUE — Reviewing scrap rates on a production line is a typical internal management accounting use for a logistics operator.', 'TRUE — Internal detail on reviewing scrap rates on a production line goes beyond what external financial accounting provides for a logistics operator.', 'TRUE — Monitoring fuel spend across a vehicle fleet is a typical internal management accounting use for a health-care provider.'], '2/5', 46, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.047', 'Divisional Performance Reports', 'Review how return relative to risk motivates owners when they judge whether to keep capital in a business. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Published financial statements of a health-care provider are too aggregated to guide day-to-day choices about monitoring fuel spend across a vehicle fleet without supplementary internal reports.', 'When a technology company focuses on assessing whether to renew an advertising contract, the relevant figures are normally drawn from management accounting rather than from the published annual report alone.', 'Published financial statements of a technology company are too aggregated to guide day-to-day choices about assessing whether to renew an advertising contract without supplementary internal reports.', 'Each verified supplier invoice recorded by a manufacturer becomes part of the ledger that supports both statutory financial statements and selective internal management reports.', 'A branch contribution schedule prepared for managers at a manufacturer illustrates management accounting that is not normally reproduced line for line in the published annual report.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Internal detail on monitoring fuel spend across a vehicle fleet goes beyond what external financial accounting provides for a health-care provider.', 'TRUE — Assessing whether to renew an advertising contract is a typical internal management accounting use for a technology company.', 'TRUE — Internal detail on assessing whether to renew an advertising contract goes beyond what external financial accounting provides for a technology company.', 'TRUE — Documentary evidence such as a supplier invoice feeds the shared transaction base used by a manufacturer for external and internal reporting.', 'TRUE — Internal formats such as a branch contribution schedule serve managers at a manufacturer without replacing statutory financial statements.'], '3/5', 47, 'full' ),
( '6.4', 'CASE 6.4.048', 'Weekly Internal Packs Versus Yearly Filings', 'Analyze why suppliers worry about a customer''s ability to pay invoices when credit terms are offered. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A branch contribution schedule prepared for managers at a retailer illustrates management accounting that is not normally reproduced line for line in the published annual report.', 'A product margin comparison prepared for managers at a service firm illustrates management accounting that is not normally reproduced line for line in the published annual report.', 'It is accurate to say that for a manufacturer, internal cost schedules are legally filed alongside the balance sheet for every business.', 'A overhead absorption worksheet prepared for managers at a construction contractor illustrates management accounting that is not normally reproduced line for line in the published annual report.', 'A rolling cash forecast prepared for managers at a hospitality group illustrates management accounting that is not normally reproduced line for line in the published annual report.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Internal formats such as a branch contribution schedule serve managers at a retailer without replacing statutory financial statements.', 'TRUE — Internal formats such as a product margin comparison serve managers at a service firm without replacing statutory financial statements.', 'FALSE — That claim about a manufacturer is incorrect and reverses the usual roles of financial and management accounting.', 'TRUE — Internal formats such as a overhead absorption worksheet serve managers at a construction contractor without replacing statutory financial statements.', 'TRUE — Internal formats such as a rolling cash forecast serve managers at a hospitality group without replacing statutory financial statements.'], '5/5', 48, 'full' ),
