( '6.5', 'CASE 6.5.001', 'Four Lenses of Financial Analysis', 'Review the four broad purposes of financial statement analysis: liquidity, profitability, financial efficiency and financial structure. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Working capital for a supermarket chain is calculated by subtracting current liabilities from current assets.', 'A supermarket chain should normally prefer positive working capital so that current assets exceed current liabilities after short-term debts are considered.', 'The current ratio of a supermarket chain compares total current assets with total current liabilities.', 'When interpreting return on capital employed for a supermarket chain, comparing the figure with similar businesses or with its own past years adds meaning.', 'A supermarket chain with negative working capital always has more cash than it needs for daily operations.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — The standard working capital definition applies to a supermarket chain: current assets minus current liabilities.', 'TRUE — Positive working capital is generally desirable for a supermarket chain because it leaves a cushion after meeting short-term obligations.', 'TRUE — Current ratio analysis for a supermarket chain uses the current assets to current liabilities relationship.', 'TRUE — Return on capital employed for a supermarket chain is most useful comparatively rather than in isolation.', 'FALSE — Negative working capital means current liabilities exceed current assets and does not imply excess cash.'], '2/5', 1, 'full' ),
( '6.5', 'CASE 6.5.002', 'Liquidity and Solvency', 'Analyze why ratio results should be interpreted alongside industry context or a business''s own history. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The acid-test ratio for a supermarket chain includes inventory in current assets before dividing by current liabilities.', 'Return on equity for a supermarket chain relates profit before interest and tax to the equity shown on the balance sheet.', 'A single return on capital employed figure for a supermarket chain is always meaningful without comparing it to other years or similar firms.', 'Asset turnover for a supermarket chain relates revenue to the average total assets employed during the year.', 'The equity ratio for a supermarket chain shows what proportion of total assets is financed by equity.'], ARRAY[false, true, false, true, true], ARRAY['FALSE — The acid-test ratio excludes inventory to provide a stricter liquidity test.', 'TRUE — Return on equity links owner capital to profit before interest and tax for a supermarket chain.', 'FALSE — Return on capital employed gains meaning chiefly from comparisons over time or with peers.', 'TRUE — Asset turnover measures how much revenue a supermarket chain generates per unit of average assets.', 'TRUE — Equity ratio analysis for a supermarket chain expresses equity as a share of total assets.'], '5/5', 2, 'full' ),
( '6.5', 'CASE 6.5.003', 'Working Capital Defined', 'Consider a listed manufacturer whose shareholders compare its return on equity with sector peers. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The debt ratio for a supermarket chain shows what proportion of total assets is financed by liabilities.', 'Working capital for a consulting firm is calculated by subtracting current liabilities from current assets.', 'A consulting firm should normally prefer positive working capital so that current assets exceed current liabilities after short-term debts are considered.', 'The current ratio of a consulting firm compares total current assets with total current liabilities.', 'Return on equity for a supermarket chain is calculated using only the cash balance rather than total equity.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Debt ratio analysis for a supermarket chain expresses total liabilities relative to total assets.', 'TRUE — The standard working capital definition applies to a consulting firm: current assets minus current liabilities.', 'TRUE — Positive working capital is generally desirable for a consulting firm because it leaves a cushion after meeting short-term obligations.', 'TRUE — Current ratio analysis for a consulting firm uses the current assets to current liabilities relationship.', 'FALSE — Return on equity uses profit before interest and tax relative to equity, not cash alone.'], '5/5', 3, 'full' ),
( '6.5', 'CASE 6.5.004', 'Positive Working Capital Preference', 'Analyze why positive working capital is generally preferred to negative working capital. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['When interpreting return on capital employed for a consulting firm, comparing the figure with similar businesses or with its own past years adds meaning.', 'Return on equity for a consulting firm relates profit before interest and tax to the equity shown on the balance sheet.', 'Asset turnover for a consulting firm relates revenue to the average total assets employed during the year.', 'The equity ratio for a consulting firm shows what proportion of total assets is financed by equity.', 'Working capital for a manufacturer is calculated by subtracting current liabilities from current assets.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Return on capital employed for a consulting firm is most useful comparatively rather than in isolation.', 'TRUE — Return on equity links owner capital to profit before interest and tax for a consulting firm.', 'TRUE — Asset turnover measures how much revenue a consulting firm generates per unit of average assets.', 'TRUE — Equity ratio analysis for a consulting firm expresses equity as a share of total assets.', 'TRUE — The standard working capital definition applies to a manufacturer: current assets minus current liabilities.'], '2/5', 4, 'full' ),
( '6.5', 'CASE 6.5.005', 'Current Ratio Explained', 'Review how the current ratio compares current assets with current liabilities. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['A manufacturer should normally prefer positive working capital so that current assets exceed current liabilities after short-term debts are considered.', 'Inventory turnover for a supermarket chain is calculated by dividing revenue by average inventory rather than using cost of sales.', 'The current ratio of a manufacturer compares total current assets with total current liabilities.', 'When interpreting return on capital employed for a manufacturer, comparing the figure with similar businesses or with its own past years adds meaning.', 'A higher inventory turnover at a supermarket chain always means more money is tied up in stock for longer periods.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Positive working capital is generally desirable for a manufacturer because it leaves a cushion after meeting short-term obligations.', 'FALSE — Inventory turnover uses cost of sales relative to average inventory, not revenue.', 'TRUE — Current ratio analysis for a manufacturer uses the current assets to current liabilities relationship.', 'TRUE — Return on capital employed for a manufacturer is most useful comparatively rather than in isolation.', 'FALSE — Higher inventory turnover indicates faster stock rotation and less money tied up, not more.'], '5/5', 5, 'full' ),
