( '6.5', 'CASE 6.5.037', 'Sector-Specific Liquidity Norms', 'Review how return on equity highlights profit relative to owners'' capital. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Liquidity analysis for a supermarket chain asks whether bills and short-term debts can be paid on time from available resources.', 'Profitability analysis for a supermarket chain judges whether profit is adequate relative to equity or capital employed rather than in isolation.', 'Financial structure analysis for a supermarket chain examines how assets are financed between equity and borrowed funds.', 'If a consulting firm borrows additional short-term funds to pay suppliers, its cash may rise while working capital can fall because current liabilities increase.', 'A current ratio between one and a half and two is often described as a comfortable liquidity range, though sector norms still matter.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — Solvency in the short run is the focus of liquidity analysis for a supermarket chain.', 'TRUE — Profit size alone is insufficient without relating it to capital for a supermarket chain.', 'TRUE — The equity-debt mix is the focus of structure analysis for a supermarket chain.', 'TRUE — Short-term borrowing can boost cash yet reduce working capital for a consulting firm.', 'TRUE — The one-and-a-half to two band is a common guideline for the current ratio when context is considered.'], '4/5', 37, 'full' ),
( '6.5', 'CASE 6.5.038', 'Asset Turnover and Expansion', 'Analyze why service businesses may show different typical current ratios from manufacturers. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Ratio analysis should combine liquidity, profitability, financial efficiency and financial structure rather than relying on one measure alone.', 'Comparing ratio figures with businesses in unrelated industries can mislead because typical structures differ widely.', 'Improving working capital at a supermarket chain sustainably may involve raising long-term equity rather than repeated overdrafts rather than relying on another short-term overdraft alone.', 'Improving working capital at a supermarket chain sustainably may involve collecting receivables faster without cutting sales rather than relying on another short-term overdraft alone.', 'A single return on capital employed figure for a online retailer is always meaningful without comparing it to other years or similar firms.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — The four analysis purposes complement one another in a full review.', 'TRUE — Industry context is essential when benchmarking ratios.', 'TRUE — Raising long-term equity rather than repeated overdrafts is a structural way to strengthen working capital at a supermarket chain.', 'TRUE — Collecting receivables faster without cutting sales is a structural way to strengthen working capital at a supermarket chain.', 'FALSE — Return on capital employed gains meaning chiefly from comparisons over time or with peers.'], '5/5', 38, 'full' ),
